<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.005      Definitions</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.008      Administration.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.015      Application of chapter to
employees and positions in executive branch.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.020      Salary table.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.023      Salary limitations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.025      Classification plan.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.027      Pay on initial employment.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.029      Guidelines for pay increases.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.030      Reclassification and resulting
pay.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.035      Pay on promotion.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.037      Special duty assignment.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.039      Working out of classification.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.041      Bilingual pay premiums.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.050      Designations or professional
licenses.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.055      Reduction of salaried
employee's salary during emergency budget crisis or financial emergency.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.065      Collective bargaining agreement
takes precedent on certain provisions if differs from chapter - limitations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.200      Chapter not a contract.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>3.15.005 
Definitions.</b>  The definitions in K.C.C. 3.12.010 apply to this chapter. 
(Ord. 14233 § 3, 2001). </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.008 
Administration.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The administration of the pay provisions set forth
herein shall be the responsibility of the county executive and shall apply to
all employees and positions in the executive branch.  (Ord. 19953 § 67, 2025: 
Ord. 19953 § 66, 2025:  Ord. 7996 § 12, 1987:  Ord. 7394 § 12, 1985:  Ord. 6627
§ 17, 1983:  Ord. 6142 § 13, 1982: Ord. 5443 § 12, 1981:  Ord. 4871 § 14,
1980:  Ord. 4160 § 13, 1979:  Ord. 3632 § 13, 1978:  Ord. 3083 § 14, 1977: 
Ord. 2685 § 14, 1976:  Ord. 2277 § 13, 1975:  Ord. 2179 § 13, 1974:  Ord. 1892
§ 13, 1974:  Ord. 1282 § 6, 1972.  Formerly K.C.C. 3.15.060).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.015  Application
of chapter to employees and positions in executive branch.</span></b>  <span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Unless
another branch of county government is specifically referenced, this chapter
shall only apply to employees and positions in the executive branch.  (Ord.
19953 § 68, 2025).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.020  Salary
table.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Except as otherwise provided by ordinance, the salary
table shall consist of ninety-nine pay ranges, each containing ten steps as
approved by ordinance annually.  (Ord. 19953 § 69, 2025:  Ord. 18727 § 17,
2018:  <span style='color:black'>Ord. 18635 </span></span><span lang=DE
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black;letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>§</span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
color:black;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>17, 2017:  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord.
18618 </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 92, 2017:  Ord. 14801 § 3, 2003:  Ord. 14233 § 4, 2001: 
Ord. 14012 § 1, 2000:  Ord. 12014 § 50, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>3.15.023 
Salary limitations</b>.  Except for annual step merit increases provided for in
this chapter or as otherwise provided by ordinance, no employee's salary shall
be greater than the amount applicable to the top step of the pay range assigned
to the employee's classification.  (Ord. 19953 § 71, 2025:  Ord. 19953 § 70,
2025:  Ord. 12014 § 54, 1995.  Formerly K.C.C. 3.15.110).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>3.15.025 
Classification plan</b>.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  The director
of the department of human resources shall develop and maintain a
classification plan for all executive branch positions.  The plan shall provide
that all positions that are substantially similar as to kind, difficulty, and
responsibility of work are included in the same classification.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  The
classification plan should set forth for each career service classification a
title, a summary of the work performed, distinguishing characteristics,
representative examples of duties, and the requirements necessary to perform
the work.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  The director
of the department of human resources:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  May
create, amend, or abolish classifications;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Should
periodically review the classification plan; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Should
assess market conditions and determine whether to make adjustments, if needed,
to pay ranges assigned to existing classifications.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.1.  The
director may assign pay ranges to new classifications and change the pay ranges
of existing classifications.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2. 
Implementation of any pay range adjustment shall be prospective and shall take
effect at the start of the pay period following the approval by the director.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  When the
pay range of a classification is increased, the incumbent employee shall be
placed at the same step in the new pay range as the employee was in the
previous pay range.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  If the
pay range of the classification decreases due to a pay range adjustment, and
the pay is the same or less than the top step of the new range, the incumbent
employee shall be placed at the step closest to their current pay rate that is
not lower than their current pay rate.  If the employee's pay rate is greater
than the highest step of the new pay range, the incumbent employee shall be
placed at the top step of the new range.  (Ord. 19953 § 72, 2025:  Ord. 18791 §
21, 2018:  Ord. 14801 § 7, 2003:  Ord. 12014 § 51, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>3.15.027 
Pay on initial employment.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.1.  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>New employees shall
start at the first step of the pay range.  If necessary for recruitment,
however, a department director may authorize an offer of a higher pay step.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  At least one of the following
criteria must be met to hire an employee above the first step:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                a.  The candidate's relevant
education and experience are significantly above the minimum requirements for
the position; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                b.  The candidate has an
especially desirable relevant skill, talent, knowledge, or ability.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  If a department director
determines it is necessary to hire an employee above the first step, a
statement of the reason for hiring the employee above the first step must be
provided to the compensation and classification services manager in the department
of human resources at the time of hire.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  The hiring of an employee above
Step 5 requires approval by the compensation and classification services
manager in the department of human resources before the hire.  (Ord. 19953 § 74,
2025:  Ord. 19953 § 73, 2025:  <span style='color:black'>Ord. 18793 </span></span><span
lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;color:black'> </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black'>24, 2018:  Ord. 18635 </span><span
lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;color:black'> </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black'>18, 2017:  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 14801 <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span>9, 2003:  Ord. 14233 § 5, 2001.  Formerly
K.C.C. 3.15.120).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.029  Guidelines
for pay increases.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Consistent with K.C.C. 3.12.350, the director shall
establish guidelines for pay increases in accordance with the following:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  Employees
may receive within-range increases from one step to the next higher step upon
satisfactory completion of the probationary period.  All probationary period
pay increases must be supported by a documented performance appraisal.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Employees
may be eligible to receive increases annually in accordance with the following
principles:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  A merit
increase must be supported by an annual documented performance appraisal
approved by the department director, and it must be maintained in the
employee's personnel file.  Merit increases shall be prospective only and shall
be effective on January 1 following the year that the appraisal was based;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  For
employees currently in Steps 1 through 4 in the pay range, the appointing
authority may grant an increase of a single step for standard performance, and
may grant an increase exceeding a single step for above-standard or outstanding
performance, as defined by the director;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  For
employees currently in Steps 5 through 7 in the pay range, the appointing
authority may grant an increase of one or more steps for above-standard
performance; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  For
employees currently in Steps 8 or 9 in the pay range, the appointing authority
may grant an increase of one step, not to exceed the top of the pay range, for
outstanding performance.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  An
appointing authority may grant an employee merit pay up to five percent above
the top step of the range for a period of twelve months, if both of the
following conditions are met:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  The
employee has been at the top step of the prior or current range for two years
before the award of the increase; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  The
employee has demonstrated continuous outstanding performance.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  All merit
increases are subject to the availability of funds.  Within-range merit
increases are not automatic but shall be given only upon the written direction
of the appointing authority, as defined in K.C.C. 3.12.010.C., within the
guidelines established by the director.  (Ord. 19953 § 75, 2025).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.030 
Reclassification and resulting pay.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            </span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A. 
The director may reclassify any position to an existing or new classification.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B. 
An employee or a group of employees may request that a position or group of
positions be reclassified for the following reasons:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
1.  The employee's position is not assigned to the appropriate classification;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
2.  A significant or gradual change has occurred in the employee's on-going
duties or responsibilities over a period of at least one-year; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
3.  A departmental reorganization or council action has caused the duties of
the position to change.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.1. 
An employee is not eligible to submit a reclassification request if:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
  a.  it has been less than twelve months since the date of a previous
classification determination for the position;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
  b.  the employee is on probation;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
  c.  the employee is on a performance improvement plan; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
  d.  the employee is asking for the reclassification of a special duty
position.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
2.  Temporary and term-limited temporary employees may not request a position
reclassification, except as noted in subsection D. of this section.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
3.  When an employee is no longer in the position for which the employee is
seeking reclassification, the department of human resources shall either deny
the employee's reclassification request or cancel the employee's appeal, or
both.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            D. 
Group classifications may be submitted if all of the employees' positions are
in the same classification in the same section of a division.  Term-limited
temporary employees may be reclassified as part of a group classification, but
only if the group includes at least one regular employee.  The director shall
evaluate each position individually, reserving the right to place individual
positions into different classifications.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            E. 
When the director reclassifies a position to a higher classification, the rate of
pay of the incumbent employee shall be increased to the first step of the pay
range of the new classification or the step that is at least five percent above
the former rate of pay, whichever is greater.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            F. 
When the director reclassifies a position to a lateral classification, rate of
pay of the incumbent employee shall remain at the same step of the pay range.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            G. 
When the director reclassifies a position to a lower classification, the rate
of pay of the incumbent employee shall be the highest step in the new pay range
that does not exceed the employee's current rate of pay.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            H. 
A pay increase as a result of a reclassification may not exceed the top step of
the new range, unless the employee's former pay includes above-Step-10 merit pay. 
If the employee's former pay includes above-Step-10 merit pay, the employee's
new pay is calculated using the above-Step-10 amount.  If the increase from
reclassification results in pay that is above the top step of the new range,
the pay shall be reduced to the top step of the new range at the end of the
incentive period, unless the employee requalifies for above-Step-10 merit award.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            I. 
Implementation of a reclassification and any related pay change shall be
effective at the start of the pay period following receipt of the completed
reclassification request form at compensation and classification services in
the department of human resources, except a reclassification to a lower pay
grade shall be effective at the start of the pay period at least thirty
calendar days after notification of the classification determination from the department
of human resources.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            J. 
A reclassified employee shall not serve a probationary period in the new
classification.  </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            K.1. 
When an employee's position is reclassified retroactively into a classification
with a different Fair Labor Standards Act status, the change in status shall be
prospective only.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
2.  When an employee's position is reclassified from a Fair Labor Standards Act
exempt classification to a Fair Labor Standards Act non-exempt classification,
the employee shall be paid overtime pay prospectively from the date of the
reclassification decision.  </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
3.  When an employee's position is reclassified from a Fair Labor Standards Act
non-exempt classification to a Fair Labor Standards Act exempt classification,
the employee shall receive a cash out of all accrued compensatory time.</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 
(Ord. 19953 § 76, 2025:  Ord. 19563 § 9, 2022:  Ord. 18793 § 22, 2018:  Ord. 18727
§ 18, 2018:  Ord. 14801 § 4, 2003:  Ord. 12014 § 52, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.15.035  Pay on promotion.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  </span><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>If a promotion results from something
other than a reclassification, the pay rate of the incumbent employee shall be
increased to the first step of the pay range of the new classification or the
step that is at least five percent above the former rate of pay, whichever is
greater.  The promoted employee may be placed at a higher step in the pay range
if the employee's department director determines the action is warranted, if
the criteria and procedures in K.C.C. 3.15.027, are met and if funds are
available in the agency.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  <span style='letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>A pay increase as a result of a promotion may not exceed the top step of
the new range, unless the employee's former pay includes an above-Step-10
amount as a result of an merit increase.  If the employee's former pay includes
above-Step-10 merit pay, the employee's new pay is calculated upon the
above-Step-10 amount.  If the increase from a promotion results in pay that is
above the top step of the new range, the pay shall be reduced to the top step
of the new range at the end of the merit period unless the employee requalifies
for an above-Step-10 merit award.</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C. 
Implementation of a promotion and any related pay change shall be prospective
and is effective when the promotion is approved by the director</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>.  (Ord. 19953 § 79,
2025:  Ord. 19953 § 78, 2025:  Ord. 18727 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span>19,
2018:  Ord. 14801 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span>10, 2003:  Ord.
14233 § 6, 2001.  Formerly K.C.C. 3.15.130).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.15.037  Special duty assignment.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.1.  A department director and,
when required, the director of the department of human resources, may assign an
employee in a regular position to an existing classification for a limited term
when the duties and responsibilities of the other classification comprise the
majority of the work performed for a minimum of thirty calendar days.  This is
called a special duty assignment.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Temporary employees,
including term-limited temporary employees, are not eligible for special duty
assignments.</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.1. 
Depending upon the type of special duty assignments needed for business
operations, special duty assignments may be made for up to a maximum of five
years.</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
2.  Assignments may be approved for up to a term of twelve months if authorized
in advance by the department director to backfill for a vacant regular
position, or to provide additional staffing needed:</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>               
a.  due to work that exceeds either the volume or complexity, or both, than
what is routinely expected, but the work is of a limited duration;</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>               
b.  due to work that is unanticipated due to unique circumstances that are not
expected to reoccur; or</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>               
c.  to either develop or implement, or both, a new function, system or
proposal.</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
3.  Assignments may be approved for up to a term of up to three years if
authorized in advance by the director to perform a significant or substantial
body of work, such as a nonroutine project or work related to the initiation or
cessation of a county function, project, or department.</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
4.  Assignments may be approved for up to a term of five years if authorized in
advance in writing by the director:</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>               
a.  to backfill a regular position, when:</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>               
  (1)  an employee is absent because of an extended leave of absence for a
medical reason;</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
    (2)  an employee is absent because of military service; or</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
    (3)  an employee is absent because of a special duty or another assignment;
and</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
  b.  to staff or backfill staff on a clearly defined grant-funded, capital
improvement or information systems technology project.</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
5.  A special duty backfill assignment may not exceed the term of the incumbent
employee's absence.</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
6.  Special duty assignments to salaried classifications shall be made in
full-week increments, from Saturday through Friday.</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
7.  An employee's special duty assignment shall end when management becomes
aware that the employee's absence will exceed thirty calendar days or at the
conclusion of a thirty-day absence, whichever occurs first.</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C. 
A special duty assignment must be made in writing to the employee before the
beginning of the assignment.  The written notice must provide the
classification title and description and must list the specific duties that the
employee is to perform and the duration of the assignment.  The written notice
must also include a statement that the assignment does not confer on the
employee any new privilege, right of appeal, right of position, transfer,
demotion, promotion or reinstatement.  A special duty assignment may be revoked
at any time at the discretion of the appointing authority.  Special duty pay
may not be assigned retroactively.</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            D. 
If the special duty assignment is to a higher-level classification, the pay
increase shall be to the first step of the pay range of the higher-level job
classification or a flat five percent above the base rate of pay, whichever is
greater.</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            E. 
If the employee was receiving above-Step-10 merit pay, the pay for the special
duty assignment is calculated using the merit pay and may result in merit pay
while in the special duty assignment.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            F. 
If an assignment is to a lateral or lower-paying classification, the employee
shall continue to receive their current rate of pay for the assignment.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            G. 
While on special duty assignment, the employee shall continue to be eligible
for step increases in the employee's regular position.  If the employee is at
Step-10 in the employee's regular position, the employee shall be eligible for
step increases in the special duty classification.</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            H. 
Any accrued compensatory time shall be cashed out before an hourly employee
begins a salaried special duty assignment, and before an employee in an hourly
special duty assignment returns to a salaried regular position.</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            I. 
When the special duty assignment is completed, the employee's pay shall revert
to the rate of pay the employee would have received if the employee had not
been assigned to special duty.</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            J. 
Special duty pay shall not be considered part of an employee's base rate of pay
for purposes of placement within a salary range as a result of promotion or
reclassification, for purposes of cashing out vacation or sick leave or when
making vacation or sick leave donations.  If the special duty position is
converted to a regular position while the employee is serving in the special
duty assignment, and the employee is promoted into the regular position, the
employee's rate of pay shall not be lower than the rate of pay the employee
received during the special duty assignment.  The promoted employee may be
placed at a higher step in the pay range if the employee's department director
determines the action is warranted based on the criteria in K.C.C. 3.15.027.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            K. 
When the special duty assignment is hourly, the employee's special duty pay shall
be used for the computation of overtime and compensatory time.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            L.
 If the special duty position is converted to a regular position and the
employee who served in the special duty position is hired into the regular
position</span> <span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>within
one year of serving in the special duty assignment, the time served in the
special duty position shall count toward any required probationary period.  If
the time served in the special duty position was longer than the required
probationary period, the employee's probationary period shall be considered
served.</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'> 
(Ord. 19953 § 82, 2025: Ord. 19738 § 10 , 2023 (expired): Ord. 17020 § 2, 2011:
Ord. 14801 § 11, 2003: Ord. 14233 § 7, 2001.  Formerly K.C.C. 3.15.140).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.15.039 
Working out of classification.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A. 
For the purposes of this section, &quot;working out of classification&quot;
means an employee in a regular position is assigned in writing some or all of
the duties of a higher-paid classification for a period of less than thirty
calendar days.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B. 
Working-out-of-classification assignments must occur in full day or full shift
increments.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C. 
While working out of classification, the employee shall receive a flat five
percent pay premium.  Any overtime the employee earns while working out of
classification shall include the five percent premium.  Paid leaves taken while
an employee is working out of classification shall not include the pay premium.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.5in;text-align:justify;text-autospace:
none'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            D. 
If a working-out-of-classification assignment exceeds twenty-nine consecutive
calendar days, the assignment shall be prospectively converted to a special
duty assignment.  (Ord. 19953 § 83, 2025:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 18727 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span>
21, 2018.  Formerly K.C.C. 3.15.145).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.041  Bilingual
pay premiums.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A. 
Nonrepresented employees in positions designated by the department of human
resources to have non-English language or American Sign Language proficiency as
a desired or required skill for the position are eligible to receive bilingual
pay premiums.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Eligible
employees whose job posting or other written notice for the position include as
a desired skill the use of one or more specific non-English language or
American Sign Language will receive a one dollar per hour premium on actual
hours worked.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  Eligible
employees whose job posting or other written notice for the position includes
as a required skill the use of one or more specific non-English language or
American Sign Language will receive a two dollar per hour premium on actual
hours worked.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  To qualify
for the bilingual pay premium, eligible employees must demonstrate sufficient
language proficiency as determined by the county.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  The
department of human resources may end or modify the assignment of job duties
and the designation of a position that qualifies for bilingual pay premiums at
any time, which will result in the immediate termination of the bilingual pay
premium.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F.  Any
employee deemed ineligible for a bilingual pay premium shall not be required to
provide bilingual services.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            G.  Bilingual
pay premiums for represented employees shall be subject to the employees'
applicable collective bargaining agreement.  Unless otherwise provided in an  </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>applicable collective
bargaining agreement, the bilingual pay premium shall not apply to represented
employees.  (Ord. 19990 § 3, 2025).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.15.050 
Designations or professional licenses.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  An employee who has a
valid Washington State Professional Civil Engineering license, a registered
architect's license, or a professional designation of CPA, MAI, RM, SSA, CPM,
or SR/WA, shall be paid an additional twenty-five dollars per month if such a
designation or professional license is a requirement of the job assignment. (Ord.
19953 § 84, 2025:  Ord. 7996 § 11, 1987:  Ord. 7394 § 11, 1985:  Ord. 6627 §
16, 1983:  Ord. 6142 § 12, 1982:  Ord. 5443 § 11, 1981:  Ord. 4871 § 13, 1980: 
Ord. 4160 § 12, 1979:  Ord. 3632 § 12, 1978:  Ord. 3083 § 13, 1977:  Ord. 2685
§ 13, 1976: Ord. 2277 § 12, 1975:  Ord. 2179 § 12, 1974:  Ord. 1892 § 12,
1974:  Ord. 1780 § 3, 1973).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.15.055  Reduction of salaried
employee's salary during emergency budget crisis or financial emergency.</b>  </span><span
lang=X-NONE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>The
director may reduce a salaried employee's salary during an &quot;emergency
budget crisis&quot; or &quot;financial emergency,&quot; as defined in K.C.C.
3.12F.010.  Such a salary reduction shall not be considered a demotion or a
layoff and shall not extend beyond the end of the emergency budget crisis or
financial emergency.</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'> 
(Ord. 19953 § 85, 2025:  Ord. 19197 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span>5,
2020.  Formerly K.C.C. 3.15.135).</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.15.065 
Collective bargaining agreement takes precedent on certain provisions if
differs from chapter - limitations.</b></span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A. 
When a collective bargaining agreement establishes a condition of employment,
benefit, or procedure that differs with a condition, benefit, or procedure
established by this chapter, the collective bargaining agreement shall take
precedence with respect to those employees covered by the agreement, so long as
the following conditions are met:</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
1.  The condition of employment, benefit, or procedure created by the agreement
is lawful; and</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>             
2.  The agreement has been adopted by the council by ordinance.</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B. 
Adoption of the agreement by ordinance shall be deemed an amendment of this
chapter only with respect to the affected employees and subject condition,
benefit, or procedure.  (Ord. 19953 § 87, 2025).</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.15.200 
Chapter not a contract.</b>  This chapter does not constitute an express or
implied contract. It is a general statement of county policy that cannot form
the basis of a private right of action.  (Ord. 14801 § 12, 2003).</span></p>

<p class=Flush1CS1><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc94867920"></a><a name="_Toc151386329"><span style='font-size:
12.0pt'>3.16    LABOR AND EMPLOYEE RELATIONS</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.010      Bargaining agent designated.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.012      Mission.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.015      Definitions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.020      Powers of bargaining agent.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.025      General provisions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.030      Prior acts confirmed.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.040      Time limit.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.045      Corrections officers.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.050      Labor policy committee
functions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.055      Complaints about conduct –
procedures – reports – review of agreements – confidentiality and guidelines.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.060      Annual labor summit.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.100      Unclassified and exempt
employees - sheriff's employees - designation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.010 
Bargaining agent designated.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  In accordance with
Section 890 of the King County Charter, the King County executive is the
designated bargaining agent for King County.  (Ord. 19953 § 88, 2025:  Ord.
19741 § 30, 2024:  Ord. 17233 § 2, 2011:  Ord. 197 § 1, 1969).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.012 
Mission.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The mission of the council and the bargaining agent
shall be to develop labor relations policy and other policies affecting county
employees in accordance with the following principles:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  Provide a
positive climate in King County government where employees feel their
contributions are valued, their ideas are heard, and their desires to serve the
public are fulfilled;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Help
county employees view King County government as a desirable place to work and
as a place where the public business is conducted in a cost-effective manner;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  Allow the
council an adequate and meaningful opportunity to provide policy direction to
the bargaining agent before the collective bargaining process begins;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  Cause King
County management to plan, prepare and be accountable for obtaining agreements
at the bargaining table concerning operating improvements necessary to best
serve the public interest and improve the working conditions for employees;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  Create and
maintain a collective bargaining and employee relations climate in King County
government that encourages cooperative efforts and joint problem-solving among
bargaining representatives, the bargaining agent, employees and management to
address ways to better serve the public, increase productivity, reduce waste,
improve safety, improve morale, and recruit and retain quality employees; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F. 
Acknowledge, encourage, and continue the efforts of bargaining units and
management to engage in collaborative or interest-based bargaining, that has
had the positive effects of reducing the adversarial nature of traditional
bargaining and enhancing consensus-making in labor relations.  (Ord. 19953 § 89,
2025:  Ord. 11480 § 5, 1994).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.015 
Definitions.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Unless the text clearly indicates otherwise, as used in
this chapter, the following words shall have the meanings set forth in this
section:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  &quot;Bargaining
agent&quot; means the designated bargaining agent as determined under K.C.C.
3.16.010.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B. 
&quot;Bargaining representative&quot; means any lawful organization which has
as one of its primary purposes the representation of employees in their
employment relations with King County.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  &quot;Commission&quot;
means the Public Employment Relations Commission.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D. 
&quot;Corrections officer&quot; means any full-time, fully compensated
uniformed correctional officer or sergeant who works for the department of
adult and juvenile detention.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  &quot;Executive
director&quot; means the executive director of the commission.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F.  &quot;Labor
policy&quot; or &quot;policy&quot; means those general principles that work to
implement  the  intent  of this  chapter  and guide  negotiations  for  wages,
benefits, working conditions, and other terms of employment.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            G. 
&quot;Labor policy committee&quot; or &quot;policy committee&quot; means the
King County council.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            H.  &quot;911
operator&quot; means any full-time, fully compensated communications specialist
or communications specialist supervisor who works for the department of public
safety.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            I. 
&quot;Public employer&quot; means King County.  (Ord. 19953 § 90, 2025:  Ord. 19013
§ 6, 2019:  Ord. 17223 § 3, 2011:  Ord. 17024 § 2, 2011:  Ord. 14287 § 1,
2002:  Ord. 11480 § 3, 1994:  Ord. 10631 § 2, 1992).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.020  Powers
of bargaining agent.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The bargaining agent is authorized on behalf of King
County to meet, confer and negotiate with bargaining representatives of the
public employees of King County for the purpose of collective bargaining as
contemplated by chapter 41.56 RCW and Section 890 of the King County Charter,
and to timely recommend to the King County council proposed wages, hours, and
other conditions of county employment for the purpose of such a collective
bargaining agreement or agreements as may be required and authorized by
ordinance.  For the purpose of this section, &quot;wages&quot; includes leaves
and employee benefits.  The bargaining agent shall not negotiate new collective
bargaining agreements prior to preparing for bargaining and conferring with the
labor policy committee as required in K.C.C. 3.16.012, 3.16.025, and 3.16.050. 
(Ord. 19953 § 91, 2025:  Ord. 14287 § 2, 2002:  Ord. 11480 § 4, 1994:  Ord. 197
§ 2, 1969).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.025 
General provisions.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            </span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A. 
The bargaining agent shall establish and conduct a process to prepare for
negotiations that performs at least the following functions:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  The
bargaining agent should continue to use collaborative or interest-based
bargaining where both parties agree, and this chapter shall not be construed to
restrict or inhibit such bargaining;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  The
bargaining agent shall cause to be developed and maintained a database of
information within King County government on wages, hours, employee benefits,
vacation and other leave, job classifications, and substantial and factual
information to provide knowledge of working conditions necessary to conduct
effective negotiations.  Such information shall be made available to the
bargaining representatives to the extent provided by RCW 41.56.030(4), in the
Public Employees' Collective Bargaining Act; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  The labor
policy committee shall confer with the bargaining agent to develop necessary
guidelines for the implementation of this section, consistent with this
chapter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  The
bargaining agent shall be the sole negotiator for King County government and
shall bargain in good faith as provided by law.  The bargaining agent shall
commence and complete collective bargaining negotiations in a timely manner and
in accordance with the overall principles and intent of this chapter.  (Ord.
19953 § 92, 2025:  Ord. 19013 § 7, 2019:  Ord. 14287 § 3, 2002:  Ord. 11480 §
7, 1994).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.030 
Prior acts confirmed.  </span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Any act pursuant to the authority and
prior to October 30, 1969, is hereby ratified and confirmed. (Ord. 197 § 3,
1969).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.040  Time
limit - payment of interest.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            </span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A. 
Any collective bargaining agreement between King County and a recognized
bargaining representative as defined in RCW 41.56.030 which has been signed by
the union shall be transmitted to the King County council no later than fourteen
days after receipt by the executive's office.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Failure to
meet the deadline in subsection A. of this section shall result in the payment
of interest on the retroactive amount of any negotiated salary or wage increase
equal to interest earned on federal ninety-day treasury bills from the first
day following the deadline through the date the signed agreement is transmitted
to the King County council, unless the fourteen days have been extended by
mutual agreement by both parties in writing.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  The
interest accrued, if any, shall be divided among the county employees
represented by the collective bargaining unit, based upon each employee’s
individual retroactive wage rate increase.  The computed interest shall be
included in the first paycheck that pays out the rate of pay negotiated in the
tentative collective bargaining agreement.  (Ord. 20082 § 24, 2026:  Ord. 19953
§ 93, 2025:  Ord. 1903 § 8, 2019:  Ord. 8658 § 1, 1988).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.045 
Corrections officers</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>.  Application of RCW 41.56.440 - 41.56.470 and RCW
41.56.480 - 41.56.490.  In the furtherance of collective bargaining the
provisions of RCW 41.56.440 - 41.56.470 and RCW 41.56.480 - 41.56.490 shall
also be applicable to corrections officers and 911 operators as defined in this
chapter.  (Ord. 10631 § 3, 1992).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.050 
Labor policy committee functions.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  The labor
policy committee shall meet as it deems necessary to obtain the testimony of
members of the public, the bargaining agent, bargaining representatives or
their designees, county department management, and others in order to consider
such testimony in policy decisions before the committee.  The labor policy
committee shall not engage in bargaining with bargaining representatives or
represented employees.  The labor policy committee shall also meet to consider
matters referred to it by the council in accordance with K.C.C. chapter 1.24.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  The labor
policy committee shall provide an opportunity for bargaining representatives or
their designees to address the committee before the adoption of overall
policy.  Overall policy, and all amendments to adopted policies, shall be
established only upon an affirmative vote by a majority of the members of the
labor policy committee.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  The
bargaining agent shall recommend to the labor policy committee overall changes
to adopted policies that would be required to implement the changes proposed in
K.C.C. 3.16.055.C., and an overall estimate of the monetary value, if any, of
these changes, including both costs and benefits.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  Following
the establishment of overall policy, and before commencing negotiations, the
labor policy committee shall meet to hear the bargaining agent's recommended
strategies for implementing adopted policies.  The labor policy committee shall
confer with the bargaining agent as it deems necessary to ensure compliance
with this chapter and good-faith collective bargaining.  The bargaining agent's
strategies shall be generally consistent with the principals contained in this
chapter and the overall policy direction established by the labor policy
committee.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  The
bargaining agent may seek further clarification of adopted policies from the
labor policy committee at any time during the negotiations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F.  By June 30
of each year, the executive shall report to the labor policy committee
regarding employment policies applicable to nonrepresented employees.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            G.  For the
purpose of maintaining an effective collective bargaining process, the
strategies and related information presented by the bargaining agent shall be
maintained as confidential.  In addition, proposed or adopted policies
designated as confidential shall be considered policy formulation documents and
be maintained as confidential and exempt from public disclosure as provided in
RCW 42.56.280.  The labor policy committee shall develop guidelines to assist
in accomplishing such confidentiality.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            H.  Any
councilmember may propose the adoption, amendment or repeal of any labor policy
by filing with the clerk of the council a memorandum that includes the proposed
policy.  Any proposed amendment shall set for the existing policy and show
proposed changes as in the form required for ordinances by K.C.C. 1.24.075. 
The clerk shall provide a copy of the proposal to the executive, each
councilmember, and the lead staff for the labor policy committee.  The proposal
shall be designated by the councilmember, either as public or as confidential
pending action by the committee on the policy.  Adopted policies may be
designated as confidential by an affirmative vote of a majority of the members
of the policy committee.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            I.  The clerk
of the council shall maintain a compilation of adopted policies.  The clerk
shall make publicly available all public policies, and shall maintain as
confidential all labor policies designated as confidential policy formulation
documents.  (Ord. 19953 § 94, 2025:  <span style='color:black'>Ord. 19013 </span></span><span
lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;color:black;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black;letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>9, 2019:  Ord. 18635 </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
color:black;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>19, 2017:  </span><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 17262 § 1,
2012:  Ord. 14287 § 4, 2002:  Ord. 12014 § 55, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>3.16.055  Complaints
about conduct – procedures – reports – review of agreements – confidentiality
and guidelines.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.1.  A
bargaining representative may at any time during negotiations forward to the labor
relations director in the executive's office, or successor, a written complaint
that the collective bargaining process is not being conducted in a timely
manner or is not being conducted in a manner consistent with good faith
bargaining.  The labor relations director in the executive's office shall,
within fifteen calendar days, respond in writing to the complaint and propose
such remedies as may address the complaint.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  If the
bargaining representative is not satisfied with the written response of the director,
or if a written response to the complaint is not received within fifteen
calendar days, the bargaining representative may forward the written complaint
to the King County executive, as the bargaining agent, who shall, within
fifteen calendar days, respond to it in writing and propose such remedies as
may address the complaint.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  If the
bargaining representative is not satisfied with the written response of the
bargaining agent, or if a written response is not received from the bargaining
agent within fifteen calendar days, the bargaining representative may request
that the bargaining agent forward the written complaint to the council.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.1in;text-align:justify'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>             
4.  If the bargaining agent receives a written request to have the complaint
forwarded to the council, including an explanation of reasons for the request,
the bargaining agent shall forward the request, together with the bargaining
agent's written response, to the council within five calendar days from the
receipt of the request.  These materials or any discussion thereof shall remain
confidential to the extent allowed by law.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-right:.1in;text-align:justify'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>             
5.  The council may request that the bargaining agent meet with the council for
the purpose of reviewing the status of negotiations with regard to the
principles contained in this chapter and the overall policy direction
established by the labor policy committee, but the council shall take no action
that would interfere with the lawful role of the bargaining agent.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  By June 30
of each year, the prosecuting attorney, in conjunction with bargaining agent,
shall report to the council on all pending unfair labor practice charges and
all pending arbitration involving represented employees.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.  By June 30 of each year, or, in
the case of agreements expiring other than December 31, at least ninety days
before the commencement of negotiations, in preparation for collective
bargaining the bargaining agent shall report to the council the agreements
expiring that calendar year.  The <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>bargaining</span>
<span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>agent</span> shall also generally explain
existing policies that, if changed, would further the principles and intent
established by this chapter.  County department management concerned with the
collective bargaining process, with the advice of other relevant county
departments, shall assist the bargaining agent in reporting to the council.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  By June 30
of each year or, for agreements expiring other than December 31, at least
ninety days before commencing negotiations, the council shall meet with the
bargaining agent to review the schedule of collective bargaining agreements
expiring in that calendar year and the key issues related to the collective
bargaining process.  Methods of consultation with unions, management rights and
eliminating the causes of employee grievances shall also be considered.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>For the purpose of
maintaining an effective collective bargaining process, the strategies and
related information presented by the bargaining agent shall be maintained as
confidential.  The council shall develop guidelines to assist in accomplishing
such confidentiality.  (Ord. 20082 § 25, 2026:  Ord. 19953 § 95, 2025:  <span
style='color:black'>Ord. 19013 </span></span><span lang=DE style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black'> </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black'>10, 2019:  Ord.
18793 </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
color:black;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black'> </span><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black'>26, 2018:  Ord. 18635 </span><span
lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;color:black'> </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;color:black'>20, 2017:  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 14287 <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ </span>5, 2002).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>3.16.060 
Annual labor summit. </b>The chair of the King County council shall annually
convene a summit between the county's elected officials, the local labor
leadership and the leadership of all collective bargaining units representing
the county's work force. Such a labor summit shall take place between January 1
and July 1 of each given year. The intent of convening an annual labor summit
shall be to: increase communication between King County elected officials and
the leadership and membership of local labor organizations and of all the
county's collective bargaining units; identify issues and problems of mutual
concern; identify solutions to problems affecting the memberships of the county's
collective bargaining units; delineate ways in which the county's elected
officials may more closely and effectively work with the county's collective
bargaining units and local labor organizations to attain mutual goals; and
foster a spirit of cooperation in working to serve the public.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            Meeting
minutes at the summit shall be recorded and adopted by the King County council
at a subsequent regular meeting of the council.  (Ord. 19953 § 96, 2025:  Ord. 15196
§ 1, 2005 Ord. 14337 § 1, 2002:  Ord. 14287 § 6, 2002:  Ord. 13000 § 2, 1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.16.100 
Unclassified and exempt employees - sheriff's employees - designation.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  The county
sheriff may designate as unclassified the number of positions as allowed by and
in accordance with RCW 41.14.070(1) through (3).        B.  By the power
conferred upon the county legislative authority in RCW 41.14.070(4), the
council authorizes the county sheriff to designate as unclassified up to the
maximum number of positions of administrative responsibility allowed.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  Positions
in the sheriff's office designated as unclassified under subsections A. and B.
of this section shall be exempt from the state civil service system under
Chapter RCW 41.14.  (Ord. 19741 § 31, 2024:  Ord. 16977 § 2, 2010:  Ord. 15939
§ 3, 2007).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc94867921"></a><a name="_Toc151386330"><span style='font-size:
12.0pt'>3.18    LIVING WAGE FOR COUNTY EMPLOYEES.</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.18.010      </span><span lang=DE
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Definitions.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.18.030      </span><span lang=DE>Rules
- curing violations - sanctions or remedies - administration of other
requirements.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.18.035      County employees with
disabilities – payment of less than minimum wage prohibited.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><b><span lang=DE>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><b><span lang=DE>            3.18.010 
Definitions.</span></b></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>            A.  &quot;Employ&quot;
means to engage, suffer, or permit to work.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>            B.  &quot;Employee&quot;
means any individual employed by an employer, whether on a full-time,
part-time, temporary or seasonal basis, including temporary workers, contracted
workers, contingent workers, and persons made available to work through a
temporary services, staffing, or employment agency or similar entity.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>            C.  &quot;Hourly
minimum wage&quot; means the minimum wage due to an employee under this chapter
for each hour worked during a pay period.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>            D.  &quot;Person&quot;
means one or more individuals, partnerships, associations, organizations, corporations,
cooperatives, legal representatives, trustees, trustees in bankruptcy,
receivers, or groups or persons, limited liability companies, sole
proprietorships, joint adventures, estates, trusts, or other entities, groups
or combinations acting as units, and the individuals constituting the groups or
units.</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>            E.  &quot;Wage&quot;
means compensation due to an employee by reason of employment, payable in legal
tender of the United States or checks on banks convertible into cash on demand
at full face value.  (Ord. 19762 § 6, 2024:  Ord. 18871 § 1, 2019:  Ord. 17909
§ 4, 2014).</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><b><span lang=DE>            3.18.030 
Minimum wage for county employees - adjustment.</span></b><span lang=DE>  The
county shall pay its employees an hourly minimum wage rate of not less than
twenty dollars and twenty-nine cents per hour or the hourly minimum wage rate
established by federal or state law, whichever is greater.  Beginning January
1, 2025, the hourly minimum wage rate shall increase annually on a percentage
basis to reflect the annual rate of inflation and calculated to the nearest
cent on January 1 of each year thereafter.  For purposes of this section,
&quot;the annual rate of inflation&quot; means one hundred percent of the
annual average growth rate of the U.S. Department of Labor, Bureau of Labor Statistics
Seattle-Tacoma-Bremerton Area Consumer Price Index for urban wage earners and
clerical workers, CPI-W, or a successor index, for the twelve month period
ending in August, but the percentage increase shall not be less than zero.  An
employee is covered by this chapter for each hour the employee is performing a
measurable amount of work as a county employee.  (Ord. 19762 § 7, 2024:  Ord. 18871
§ 2, 2019:  Ord. 17909 § 6, 2014).</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><span lang=DE>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=Normal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.18.035 
County employees with disabilities – payment of less than minimum wage
prohibited.</span></b><span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The county shall not
pay any employee with a disability as defined in K.C.C. 12.16.010. less than
any applicable minimum wage.  </span><span lang=DE>(Ord. 19009 § 1, 2019).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc94867922"></a><a name="_Toc151386331"><span style='font-size:
12.0pt'>3.24    TRAVEL AUTHORIZATION AND EXPENSE REIMBURSEMENT</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.010      Definitions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.020      Persons authorized to receive
expense reimbursement.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.030      Travel authorization.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.050      General rules.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.060      Transportation costs.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.070      Lodging costs.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.080      Per diem meal and incidental
rates and refreshment costs.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.090      Miscellaneous reimbursable
expenses.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.100      Nonreimbursable expenses.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.105      Travel expense advances.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.110      Leave of absence during travel.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.120      Lobbyist per diem in lieu of
reimbursement.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.130      Expense reimbursement.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.140      Repayment of unauthorized
reimbursements.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.150      Implementation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.170      Reimbursement of moving
expenses for certain employees.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.010 
Definitions.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  All words in this chapter shall have their ordinary and
usual meanings except those defined in this section which shall have the
meaning set forth below:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  &quot;Day
travel&quot; means travel outside of the county that exceeds six hours but does
not include an overnight stay.  Travel outside of the county for six hours or
less or travel within the county is not considered day travel.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  &quot;Emergency&quot;
means the occurrence of unforeseen or exigent circumstances which may result in
harm to the public good.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  &quot;Employee&quot;
means any person who is employed in a career service position, an exempt
position, or a temporary position as defined in K.C.C. chapter 3.12, except
persons serving the county without compensation and members of boards and
commissions.  &quot;Employee&quot; includes all county elected officials.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  &quot;Essential
employee&quot; means an employee designated by their department leadership who
provides for and maintains the functions of county essential services.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  &quot;Essential
services&quot; means those services stated or implied that are required to be</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1. 
Performed by statute or executive order for the exercise of civil authority, to
maintain the safety, health and well-being of the county population, and to
sustain the county's industrial and economic base; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Other
functions as deemed essential by the heads of county agencies.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F.  &quot;Federal
lodging limit&quot; means the maximum amount a federal employee may be
reimbursed per day for lodging expenses, excluding applicable taxes, in the
respective host city for travel within the continental United States as
published in the Code of Federal Regulations, 41 CFR Sec. 301, App. A, and as
hereafter amended.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            G.  &quot;First
responder&quot; means an employee who protects lives, property, and evidence
and who provides for the restoration of order.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            H.  &quot;Government
rates&quot; means the discounted rates offered to government employees, in the
course of conducting official business, by lodging establishments, rental car
agencies and other providers of services to government employees.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>            I.  &quot;Moving
expenses&quot; means expenses incurred for transportation of family and common
household possessions, including meals and incidentals per diem, automobiles,
and lodging expenses.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            J.  &quot;Official
county business&quot; means business that relates directly to a person's work
function and benefits the county.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            K.  &quot;Overnight
travel&quot; means travel outside of the county that exceeds twelve hours and
includes an overnight stay.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            L.  &quot;Presiding
elected official&quot; means the county executive for the executive branch
departments, agencies, and offices except assessments and elections; the county
assessor for the department of assessments; the director of elections for the
department of elections, the prosecuting attorney for the office of the
prosecuting attorney; the chair of the county council for the legislative
branch; and the presiding judges of the superior and district courts, or the
official or officials designated by that branch or unit of county government.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            M.  &quot;Unanticipated
event&quot; means an event necessitating a response due to a regulator
requirement or public safety and health situation that does not rise to the
level of a proclaimed emergency.  (Ord. 19741 § 32, 2024:  Ord. 17351 § 1,
2012:  Ord. 13257 § 1, 1998:  Ord. 12904 § 2, 1997:  Ord. 9206 § 1, 1989).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.020 
Persons authorized to receive expense reimbursement.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 
This chapter applies to all departments, agencies and offices of the executive
branch, including but not limited to the departments of judicial
administration, public safety, and assessments; the council and subordinate
units of the legislative branch; the office of the prosecuting attorney; the
superior and district courts; and the office of economic and financial
analysis.  For purposes of this chapter, the presiding elected official of the
office of economic and financial analysis is the chair of the forecast
council.  Within budgetary constraints, the following persons are authorized to
receive expense reimbursement in conformance with this chapter and are likewise
responsible for compliance with the terms of this chapter and any implementing
policies and procedures:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  County
employees;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Candidates
for positions that are exempt from the career service or candidates for
positions that require a specialized skill not available in the county job
market may be authorized by the presiding elected official, subject to the
following restrictions: </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  For
travel to the county from their place of residence outside the county to be
interviewed.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Maximum
of five interview trips for a given position during any twelve month period.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  No
candidate for employment is eligible for more than two separate interview trips
for a given position.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  Approval
by the presiding elected official is required before an invitation to interview
is issued.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  Persons
otherwise authorized by law, grant or contract, provided that the terms of such
other expense reimbursement authorization shall apply.  (Ord. 16391 § 20,
2009:  Ord. 13257 § 2, 1998:  Ord. 12077 § 7, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.030 
Travel authorization.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A. 
Authorization for day or overnight travel or to incur expenses estimated to
exceed one hundred dollars shall be obtained in accordance with the established
policies and procedures of the respective presiding elected official prior to
commencing the activity.  Travel plans shall be authorized in advance by the
presiding elected official or designee, except when an emergency arises that
requires travel to commence within twenty-four hours.  If expenses are incurred
without prior to authorization, the presiding elected official or designee will
determine whether an emergency existed and the expenses are reimbursable.  The
person requesting reimbursement will be responsible for payment of expenses
that were incurred without prior authorization and subsequently determined not
to constitute an emergency.  </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Persons
authorizing travel shall not be subordinate to the requester, shall ensure that
the traveler is provided information about the applicable travel expense
reimbursement policies prior to incurring travel expenses and shall apply, at a
minimum, the criteria outlined below in making decisions on granting or denying
such requests:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1. 
Requested travel shall be for the conduct of official county business.  The
travel request shall describe the relationship between the travel and work
functions and indicate what benefit the county will receive from the travel. 
Any available printed material indicating the overall content and quality of
the event shall be attached to the travel request.  The number of persons
engaging in a given travel activity shall be the minimum necessary to achieve
the expected benefit.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Travel
requests shall be accompanied by a travel itinerary and itemized list of
anticipated expenditures. Expenditure plans must be reasonable and budgeted
monies for reimbursement must be available and authorized.  </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  For
nonmandatory attendance at job-related seminars, conferences, conventions or
training, travel may be authorized with reimbursement at less than the rates
otherwise authorized, provided that the reduced reimbursement rates are
mutually agreed to in writing by the person authorizing the travel and the
traveler prior to the travel.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  If the
person is to maintain employment/business status during the travel, travel
authorization is required even if the county will not be funding the travel
expenses. (Ord. 13257 § 3, 1998:  Ord. 9206 § 3, 1989).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.050 
General rules.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-indent:.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A.
All persons are responsible for exercising prudent judgment to avoid
unnecessary county expense.  Travel requests and reimbursement claims shall
strictly conform to the provisions of this chapter and other applicable laws,
grants, contracts or policies.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  The
traveler shall be responsible for excess costs and additional travel expenses
resulting from taking an indirect route or delaying the return trip for
personal preference or convenience, except that additional travel expenses may
be approved and reimbursed if an indirect route or delay reduces the county's
total costs.  Additional travel expenses paid under this provision are subject
to the reimbursement limitations specified in this chapter and, considering
both salary and travel expenses, must not exceed the documented savings.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  The
provisions of this chapter shall apply to both domestic and foreign travel,
except that the limits on travel expense reimbursements for transportation,
lodging, and meals and incidentals, as provided for in K.C.C. 3.24.060 through
3.24.080, shall apply only to travel within the continental United States,
which shall consist of the forty-eight contiguous states and the District of
Columbia.  Transportation, lodging, and meals and incidental expense
reimbursement for travel outside the continental United States shall be based
upon actual expenses to a maximum of the overseas rates established by the
federal General Services Administration for federal travel to the specific
location, subject to all other provisions of this chapter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  No travel
or meal expense that would not be a reimbursable expense under this chapter may
be the object of a direct expenditure of county funds. (Ord. 13257 § 5, 1998: 
Ord. 9206 § 5, 1989).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.060 
Transportation costs.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The county will pay the actual and necessary costs of
transportation of conducting official county business as follows:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>            A.  Travel
shall be by the method of transportation and route that will be most
advantageous to the county, considering the cost of transportation, other
travel expenses and salary.  The excess cost of first class or business class
fare for a given mode of travel shall not be reimbursed when less expensive
fares are available.  When a private mode of transportation is used in lieu of
a more customary form of transportation to the event site, travel expenses
shall be reimbursed at the lesser of the two costs.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  When
automobile transportation is necessary, a county vehicle or public
transportation shall be used whenever practical. When rental vehicles are
authorized, government rates must always be requested.  When use of a private
automobile is authorized, reimbursement for mileage shall be the current
Internal Revenue Service (IRS) rate per mile for business-related travel and
reimbursement for parking shall be the actual cost, except as provided by a
collective bargaining agreement, prevailing law or contract.  The department of
finance shall be responsible for administering and announcing the current IRS
mileage rate for business-related travel.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  The
starting and ending locations of travel are the official work place or the
residence, depending on the work schedule and the work status of the traveler
at the time of departure and arrival.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D. 
Transportation between a person's home and regularly assigned workplace is not
reimbursable, except that members of regional committees who are not full-time
elected officials may be reimbursed actual parking and round-trip mileage
expenses, at the current IRS mileage rate for business-related travel, between
the member's workplace or residence and the location of the regional committee
meeting. (Ord. 13257 § 6, 1998: Ord. 12077 § 8, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.070 
Lodging costs.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Lodging costs actually incurred are reimbursable only
as follows:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  Lodging
costs will be reimbursed only if a person is in overnight travel status, except
as provided in subsection D. of this section.  Government rates must always be
requested.  Lodging receipts are required.  Lodging costs in the host city may
be claimed from the night before the authorized event starts through the night
before it ends, unless reasonably priced and timely return transportation is
not available, thereby necessitating additional lodging costs.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  The
traveler shall be reimbursed for actual lodging costs incurred for single
occupancy, to a maximum of the federal lodging limit for the host city plus
taxes.  If the lodging receipt indicates a charge for double occupancy and two
persons are authorized to travel on behalf of the county, each traveler shall
be allowed one-half the double occupancy charge.  If one person is not
authorized to travel on behalf of the county, the person authorized to travel
shall be reimbursed at the single occupancy rate to a maximum of the federal
lodging limit.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  For
seminars, conferences or conventions, costs for lodging at the event site may
be authorized in excess of the federal lodging limit for the host city under
one or more of the following conditions:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  No
alternate lodging is available within a reasonable distance of the event site
which is within the federal lodging limit for the host city.  The traveler must
provide a signed statement of unavailability with the request for
reimbursement;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  The
authorized means of transportation between the alternate lodging site and the
event site would exceed the savings in lodging costs; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  The
presiding elected official or designee has authorized the excess expenditure in
writing and in advance for any exigent circumstances that might exist.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  Employees
who are not in overnight travel status may be provided either lodging paid
directly by the county or reimbursed by the county to the employ[ee] in the
event of an emergency proclaimed by the county executive, but only if:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  The
employee who is provided lodging must remain close to the worksite in order to
respond to the emergency;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  The work
being performed is critical or necessary to respond to the proclaimed
emergency; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  During
the first twenty-four hours, the lodging is approved by the presiding elected
official or designee in writing; any extension beyond the first twenty-four
hours must be approved in advance and by the presiding elected official or
designee in writing.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  First
responders and essential employees, who are not in overnight travel status who
must work extended hours during certain unanticipated events which is critical
to or in response to a regulatory requirement may be provided either lodging
paid by the county or reimbursed by the county to the employee, but only if:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  The
employee who is provided lodging must remain close to the worksite in order to
respond to the event;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  The
event requires that the work being performed is critical or necessary to meet a
regulatory requirement or to respond to a public health and safety situation
not rising to the level of a proclaimed emergency; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  During
the first twenty-four hours, the lodging is approved by the presiding elected
official or designee in writing with a brief description of the event; any
extension beyond the first twenty-four hours is approved in advance and by the
presiding elected official or designee in writing with a brief description of
the event.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F.  The
department of finance shall distribute federal lodging limits, as published in
the Code of Federal Regulations, 41 CFR Sec. 301, App. A, as rate changes
occur.  (Ord. 19033 § 3, 2019:  Ord. 18618 </span><span lang=DE
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
93, 2017:  Ord. 17351 § 2, 2012:  Ord. 13257 § 7, 1998:  Ord. 9206 § 7. 1989).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.080  Per
diem meal and incidental rates and refreshment costs.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>A.  <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>For persons traveling on official county business,
meal and incidental expenses are reimbursable at the per diem rates established
by the federal travel regulations for the host city, published annually in the
Code of Federal Regulations, 41 CFR Sec. 301, App. A.  The per diem rates
include fixed allowances for breakfast, lunch, dinner and incidental expenses,
by city.  The meal allowances include tips and gratuities.  The incidental
rates are calculated to allow for expenses such as fees and tips to baggage
carriers, concierges, hotel staff and laundry.  Reimbursement for incidentals
is authorized only for overnight travel.  Receipts are not required:</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  For day
travel, the fixed allowance per meal, as established by the federal per diem
rate, may be claimed if in travel status at the following times:  7:00 a.m. -
breakfast, 12 noon - lunch, and 6:00 p.m. - dinner;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  For
overnight travel, the per diem meal and incidental rate may be claimed.  On the
first and last days of travel, meals shall be reimbursed at the rates
established for day travel, plus incidentals; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  When the
expense of a meal is included in a registration fee, air fare or other county
expense, the per diem meal and incidental rate will be reduced by the fixed
allowance for the respective meal.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.1.  Meal
expenses incurred while a person is not in travel status are not normally
reimbursable, except that meals may be reimbursed or paid directly by the
county for official county business purposes as follows:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a.  for
staff retreats lasting more than four hours in a single day, for either single
or multiple days, not to exceed one retreat per quarter per county division or
key subordinate unit as defined by K.C.C. 2.16.100;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b.  when
an integral part of a job-related seminar, conference, convention or training
occurs during the meal, but only if the meals are approved in advance, in
writing, by the presiding elected official or designee;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                c.  when a
meeting subject to the Open Public Meetings Act, chapter 42.30 RCW, continues
through the times listed in K.C.C. 3.24.080.A.1;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                d.  for
events authorized in advance and in writing by a presiding elected official or
designee, but this authorization shall not be provided in circumstances that
violate Article VIII, Section 7 of the state Constitution, which prohibits
gifts of public funds;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                e.  for
events that rise to the level of an emergency declared or proclaimed by the
executive, but only if:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                  (1)  the
work being performed by the employee is critical or necessary to respond to the
emergency; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                  (2) 
during the first twenty-four hours, the meals are approved by the presiding
elected official or designee in writing; and an extension beyond the first
twenty-four hours must be approved in advance by the presiding elected official
or designee; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                f.  for
unanticipated events that do not rise to the level of an emergency proclaimed
by the executive, but only if:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                  (1)  the
employee who is provided meals is a designated first responder or an essential
employee;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                  (2)  the
event requires that the work being performed is critical or necessary to meet a
regulatory requirement or to respond to a public safety and health situation
not rising to a proclaimed emergency; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                  (3) 
during the first twenty-four hours, the meals are approved by the presiding
elected official or designee in writing with a brief description of the event;
and an extension beyond the first twenty-four hours is approved in advance by
the presiding elected official or designee in writing with a brief description
of the event.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2. 
Reimbursable meals incurred while in nontravel status are limited to the fixed
meal allowance established by the federal travel regulations for each
participant.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  Expenses
for refreshments are not normally reimbursable, except that refreshment
expenses may be reimbursed when an employee is not in travel status, under the
conditions provided for in K.C.C. 3.24.080.B.  Additionally, refreshments may
be provided to employees by the county, at its option.  Refreshment expenses,
however, are limited to fifty percent of the fixed lunch meal allowance
established by the federal travel regulations for each participant.  Any
purchase of refreshments that will cost more than fifty dollars in total per
function must be approved in advance and in writing by the presiding elected
official or designee.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  Meal and
refreshment costs are neither reimbursable nor may they be paid by the county
as a direct expenditure when:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  They are
included in another county expense, regardless of whether the person partakes
in the meal or refreshment;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  They are
incurred for recreational or social events such as office parties, going away
parties, retirement parties, or other personalized social events; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  It would
violate the provisions of Article VIII, Section 7 of the state Constitution,
which prohibits a gift of public funds.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  Meal
expenses may be incurred at a rate higher than that established by federal
regulations when one or more of the following conditions apply:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  The
presiding elected official or designee approves the cost of the meal because a
circumstance related to a particular meal results in exceeding the authorized
meal rate.  A receipt detailing the expense, accompanied by written
justification, shall be submitted with the request to exceed the fixed meal
allowance;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  The meal
expense is incurred on behalf of another agency that reimburses the county for
the expense, in which case the expense shall be reimbursed according to the
rules specified by the funding agency; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  When
necessitated by special dietary needs.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F.  The
finance and business operations division shall distribute federal meal and
incidental rates, as published in the Code of Federal Regulations, 41 CFR Sec.
301, App. A, as rate changes occur.  (Ord. 19033 § 4, 2019:  Ord. 13257 § 8,
1998:  Ord. 12077 § 9, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>3.24.090 
Miscellaneous reimbursable expenses.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A. 
Miscellaneous expenses related to official county business travel which are
considered essential and reimbursable include, but are not limited to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1. 
Registration fee of a seminar, conference, convention or similarly organized
program subject to the limitation in K.C.C.3.24.080A.3.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Rental
of a room in a hotel or other facility that is used to transact official
business.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  Parking;
ferry and bridge tolls; taxi, subway and bus fares; airport shuttle service and
rental vehicles.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4. 
Stenographic, typing or computer-related services.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              5. 
Telephone calls or facsimile (fax) transmissions necessary for the conduct of
official business or to advise of a change in official travel plans.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              6.  Traveler's
checks.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              7.  Baggage
handling service, when necessitated by physical limitations or when carrying
excessive baggage or equipment required for an official business purpose, may
be claimed beyond the expense limitations provided for in K.C.C. 3.24.080A.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  A
miscellaneous nontravel expense related to official county business is
reimbursable if the expense is under one hundred dollars and approved in
advance by the presiding elected official or designee. (Ord. 13257 § 9, 1998:
Ord. 9206 § 9, 1989).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.100 
Nonreimbursable personal expenses.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Miscellaneous travel
expenses </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>not
directly related to the conduct of official county business are not
reimbursable.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  Certain expenses are considered
personal and therefore nonreimbursable including, but not limited to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  Laundry, except as covered by
the incidental per diem rate provided for in K.C.C. 3.24.080.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Personal telephone calls,
except as provided for in K.C.C. 3.24.090A.5.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  Entertainment, clothing,
personal sundries and services, transportation to places of entertainment and
similar personal items.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              4.  Room service costs that
exceed the fixed rate established for the meal incurred and valet service,
except when necessitated by physical limitations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              5.  Personal &quot;trip insurance&quot;
and medical or hospital services.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              6.  Alcoholic beverages and
tobacco products.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              7.  Tips and gratuities, except
as provided for in K.C.C. 3.24.080A.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              8.  Fines and penalties.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              9.  Dependent care, except as
provided through the county employee benefits program.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  The county shall not provide
funding of travel expenses when a source of reimbursement other than the county
is specified in county, state or federal law or policy.  In cases where
reimbursement is available from another source, the county may, at the option
of the approving authority, pay the difference between what the county policy
would allow for the total travel activity and the total reimbursed from the
other source for expenses reimbursable under the provisions of this chapter,
but shall not selectively reimburse for only specific items with differing
reimbursement rates between the county and the other source.<span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  (Ord. 13257 § 10, 1998: Ord. 9206 § 10, 1989).</span></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>3.24.105 
Travel expense advances.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Whenever it becomes
necessary for an employee to incur reimbursable expenses for overnight travel,
the department of finance may make a travel expense advance if requested by the
employee.  The amount of such an advance shall not exceed the amount of anticipated
reimbursable expenses.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  Travel expense advances shall
be used only to defray reimbursable expenses incurred while conducting official
county business.  Travel expense advances shall not, under any circumstances,
be considered a personal loan to the employee and any expenditure thereof,
other than for official county business purposes, shall be considered a
misappropriation of county funds.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.  On or before the tenth day
following the close of the authorized travel period, any employee who received
a travel expense advance shall submit a fully itemized travel expense voucher,
as provided for in K.C.C. 3.24.130, accompanied by the unexpended portion of
such advance, if any, to the approving official.  The approving official shall
have an additional five calendar days to process and forward the travel voucher
and unexpended funds to the department of finance.  The department of finance
shall withhold from the employee's next paycheck any travel expense advance, or
any portion thereof, not properly accounted for or repaid to the department of
finance within the fifteen-day period. Such withholdings shall include
interest, from the date of default until paid, at the rate established by RCW
42.24.150.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            D.  County employees who are
delinquent in accounting for or repaying a prior travel expense advance shall
be ineligible for any additional advances until such time as repayment or
acceptable justification for the delinquent advance has been made.  (Ord. 13257
§ 11, 1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.110 
Leave of absence during travel.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  When leave
of absence of any kind is taken while </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>in a travel status, the exact hour of departure
and return to duty status shall be shown on the travel expense claim voucher. 
Except as provided in the following paragraph, expense reimbursement, including
transportation to and from the post of duty, shall not be granted for such
period.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-right:0in;text-align:justify'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt'>            B.  Whenever a traveler takes leave of
absence because of incapacitation due to illness or injury not due to the
employee's own misconduct, the authorized reimbursement for lodging and
subsistence may be continued during the leave period, but not to exceed in
total the authorized cost for return to the employee's official station or
residence, whichever is closer, and then back to the assignment.  (Ord. 9206 §
11, 1989).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.120 
Lobbyist per diem in lieu of reimbursement.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  Any
expense for which a county employee would otherwise be reimbursed shall be a
legal obligation of and expenditure by the county when incurred in the course 
of  &quot;lobbying&quot;  as defined in RCW 42.17.020(18),  or in the course of
providing requested information to an official or officials of another
government agency.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>            B. 
Any employee of the county, when assigned to a session of the Washington state
legislature as a full time &quot;lobbyist&quot; as defined in RCW
42.17.020(19), shall be authorized to receive, in lieu of meals and incidentals
and lodging cost reimbursements provided for in this chapter, an allowance for
each and every consecutive day of a session at a rate equivalent to that authorized
for members of the Washington state legislature in accordance with RCW
44.04.120.  (Ord. 13257 § 12, 1998:  Ord. 9206 § 12, 1989).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.130 
Expense reimbursement.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            </span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A. 
Travel expenses.  The traveler shall submit a fully itemized travel expense
voucher to obtain reimbursement for travel expenses reimbursable under the
provisions of this chapter.  Travel expense reimbursement claims shall include
the time, place, business purpose and participants in accordance with
procedures established by the presiding elected official and on forms approved
by the department of finance. </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif'> Any applicable conference, convention or seminar brochure;
airline itinerary; travel authorization(s) and justifications for exceeding the
established limits for travel expenses shall be attached to the travel expense
reimbursement claim.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>For travel to a foreign
country, the travel reimbursement claim shall show the total cost in the
foreign currency, converted to U.S. dollars.  The exchange rate shall be
determined using either the rate published in the Wall Street Journal on the
date representing the mid-point of travel, or the receipt provided by the bank
or hotel making the exchange transaction, provided it shows the exchange rate,
date and transaction fee.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>For travel to Canada,
the department of finance shall ensure that the necessary forms and records are
submitted to the appropriate Canadian government to request the goods and
services tax refund.  Requests for refunds shall be made upon completion of travel
claim and voucher review.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Nontravel
expenses.  The employee shall submit a fully itemized expense voucher to obtain
reimbursement for reimbursable nontravel expenses.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  Receipts
prepared and issued by the service provider or copies of endorsed checks are
required wherever this chapter provides for actual cost reimbursement, except
that receipts are not required for expenses of less than ten dollars.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  Persons
responsible for approving expense reimbursement claims shall be the respective
presiding elected official or designee but shall not be subordinate to the
claimant.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  The
approving official of expense reimbursement claims shall ensure that one
political subdivision does not pay expenses properly attributed to another, in
violation of RCW 43.09.210.  (Ord. 13257 § 13, 1998:  Ord. 12077 § 10, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>3.24.140 
Repayment of unauthorized reimbursements.</b></span><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'> The department of finance shall seek
repayment of expenses from the person who was reimbursed whenever an audit or
subsequent review of travel expense reimbursements finds that such expenses
were reimbursed contrary to the provisions of this chapter. (Ord. 13257 § 14,
1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.24.150  Implementation.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-indent:.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>A.  Each presiding
elected official shall be responsible for preparing and adopting administrative
policies and procedures for submittal, approval and reimbursement of expense
claims.  Such policies and procedures shall include examples of the types of expenses
allowed and disallowed, consistent with the provisions of this chapter.  Should
a presiding elected official not adopt the necessary policies and procedures
within thirty days of December 10, 1998, the policies and procedure adopted by
the executive shall apply until the presiding elected official adopts the
necessary policies and procedures.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  The department of finance shall
be responsible for developing and disseminating the forms required to authorize
and reimburse expenses.  (Ord. 13257 § 15, 1998).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.24.170 
Reimbursement of moving expenses for certain employees</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-indent:.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A. 
Within the executive </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>branch,
including the department of judicial administration, the director of the department
of human resources may authorize in writing at the written request of the
appointing authority reimbursement of reasonable and necessary moving expenses
for the following categories of county employees:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  Persons whose appointments
require council confirmation and who have been so confirmed;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Persons appointed by the
county administrative officer to exempt positions;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  Persons appointed by the
directors of executive departments to exempt positions; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              4.  Persons hired for positions
requiring specialized knowledge, skill, or abilities that the director of the department
of human resources has confirmed in writing are not found in the local labor
market.  However, this subsection A.4. may be used no more than four times each
calendar year.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  Within the legislative branch,
the department of assessments, the department of elections, the office of the
prosecuting attorney, district court, and superior court, the presiding elected
official may authorize, in writing, the reasonable and necessary moving
expenses of employees appointed within their agencies.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.  The forecast council may
authorize, in writing, reimbursement of the reasonable and necessary moving
expenses of the chief economist.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            D.  Moving expenses may not be
reimbursed unless the following conditions are met:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  The reimbursement is
permitted under subsection A., B., or C. of this section;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  The reimbursement is
necessary to obtain the services of the individual whose moving expenses are
being reimbursed;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  The reimbursement is limited
to reasonable and necessary moving expenses, which may include the cost of
travel directly to the place of new residence following appointment;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              4.  The reimbursement to a
particular employee does not exceed a total of fifteen thousand dollars;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              5.  The reimbursement will not
create a need for a supplemental appropriation to the department, agency, or
office in which the individual is employed;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              6.  Before the appointment the
appointing authority and the appointee agreed to the reimbursement in writing;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              7.  The written reimbursement
agreement provides that if the appointee leaves county employment, either
voluntarily or involuntarily, less than two years after the appointment, the
appointee shall repay to the county, within thirty days after leaving county
employment, the entire amount of the reimbursement; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              8.  Reimbursement of specific
expenses complies with the other limitations contained in this chapter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            E.  Moving expenses may not be
reimbursed for confidential secretaries or other exempt clerical positions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            F.  To the extent that
reimbursement of moving expenses is permitted under this section, the
appointing authority may pay the third party provider of moving services
directly, rather than by reimbursing the employee, subject to any applicable
requirements concerning the county's purchase of goods and services from third
parties.  (Ord. 19741 § 33, 2024:  Ord. 18793 § 27, 2018:  Ord. 17974 § 2,
2015:  Ord. 16391 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span> 21, 2009:  Ord.
14199 § 31, 2001:  Ord. 13257 § 18, 1998:  Ord. 12014 § 56, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc94867923"></a><a name="_Toc151386332"><span style='font-size:
12.0pt'>3.28    COMPENSATION FOR USE OF PRIVATELY OWNED VEHICLES</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.28.010      Generally.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2 style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-indent:-99.0pt'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt'>            3.28.020      Amount.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.28.010 
Generally.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The executive, legislative, and judicial branches of
county government may, at their individual option, establish a system of
reimbursement on a monthly allotment basis for use of privately owned vehicles
used in connection with county business in lieu of assigned county vehicles.  (Ord.
19953 § 97, 2025:  Ord. 1902 § 1, 1974).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.28.020 
Amount.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The compensation provided for in K.C.C. 3.28.010 shall
not exceed the average monthly charge billed by the department of executive
services for equivalent vehicles.  (Ord. 18791 § 14, 2018:  Ord. 12077 § 11,
1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc94867924"></a><a name="_Toc151386333"><span style='font-size:
12.0pt'>3.30    USE OF COUNTY VEHICLES TO COMMUTE</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.30.010      Purpose.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.30.020      Definitions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.30.030      Take-home vehicle assignment
policies and criteria.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.30.040      Authority to approve take-home
vehicle assignments.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.30.045      Commuting in county-owned
vehicle a taxable benefit.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.30.050      Record-keeping.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.30.060      Biannual reevaluation and
update of take-home vehicle assignments.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.30.070      Occasional overnight usage -
collective bargaining agreement.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.30.010 
Purpose.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The purpose of this chapter is to ensure the proper use
of public funds with regard to the county's practice of allowing employees to
commute in county owned vehicles.  The intent of this chapter is to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>            A.  Restrict
the number of county owned vehicles being used by employees to commute;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Establish
criteria and policies for evaluating and authorizing take-home vehicle
assignments;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  Require
the fleet services division of the department of executive services to document
the number of current take-home vehicle assignments;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  Require
the fleet services division of the department of executive services to develop
administrative rules for implementing the provisions of this chapter; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.        Require
the fleet services division of the department of executive services to
reevaluate all take-home vehicle assignments in accordance with the policies
and criteria established in this section.  (Ord. 19953 § 98, 2025:  Ord. 18791
§ 15, 2018:  Ord. 12077 § 12, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.30.020 
Definitions.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  For purposes of this chapter, the following terms shall
have the meanings set forth below:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  &quot;Assigned
take-home vehicle&quot; means a county-owned vehicle which is used by a county
employee for county business and for regularly commuting to and from the
employee's home and their first and last workplace of the day.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  &quot;Assigned
vehicle&quot; means a county-owned vehicle assigned to a department or county
employee for county business, but not for employee commuting to and from the employee's
home and workplace.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  &quot;Commute&quot;
or &quot;commuting&quot; means the trip from an employee's home to their first
workplace before the start of their workday, or the trip departing from the
employee's last workplace following the end of the workday.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  &quot;Emergency
response&quot; means when an employee has a primary responsibility to respond
immediately to protect life or property, or both.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  &quot;Motor
pool dispatch vehicle&quot; means a vehicle issued from a central motor pool
for a single trip or for less than three working days.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F.  &quot;Occasional
overnight vehicle use&quot; means when a county employee takes a county-owned
vehicle home after attending night meetings or other county business activities
that occur outside an employee's normally scheduled work hours.  Occasional
overnight vehicle use shall mean no more than twelve times per quarter on
average.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            G. 
&quot;Workplace&quot; means the office or site a county employee reports to
perform normally scheduled work.  (Ord. 19953 § 99, 2025:  Ord. 11183 § 1,
1993).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.30.030 
Take-home vehicle assignment policies and criteria.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 
The council wishes to restrict the number of take-home vehicles provided to
county employees.  To accomplish this objective, the following policies and
criteria shall be used as the basis for authorizing take-home vehicle
assignments:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.1.  For
county business before or after normal working hours, providing short-term
motor pool dispatch vehicles or travel reimbursement is preferred over the
assignment of take-home vehicles.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  The
assignment of a take-home vehicle is neither a privilege, nor a right of any
county employee.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3. 
Take-home vehicle assignments shall not be made based on employee merit or
employee status.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  Wherever
possible, county vehicles shall be picked up and dropped off at designated
county parking areas, thereby avoiding the assignment of take-home vehicles;
and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.1.a. 
Take-home vehicles may be assigned to county employees who:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                  (1) 
have primary responsibility to respond to emergency situations that require
immediate response to protect life or property;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                  (2) 
respond to emergencies at least twelve times per quarter;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                  (3) 
cannot use alternative forms of transportation to respond to emergencies; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                  (4) 
cannot pick up county-owned assigned vehicles at designated sites.</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>                b.  Emergency
response assignments shall be supported by data demonstrating the actual number
and nature of emergency responses in the prior year, and estimates of future
emergency responses.  In addition, there must be an explanation why an employee
cannot use alternative forms of transportation to respond to the emergencies or
pick up county owned assigned vehicles at designated parking areas.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2. 
Take-home vehicles may be assigned if employee travel reimbursement costs are consistently
greater than the commuting costs associated with overnight vehicle usage.  In
addition, there must be an explanation why an employee cannot use alternative
forms of transportation or pick up county owned vehicles at designated parking
areas.  (Ord. 19953 § 100, 2025:  Ord. 15988 § 2, 2007:  Ord. 11183 § 2, 3,
1993:  Ord. 10930 § 3-4, 1993).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.30.040 
Authority to approve take-home vehicle assignments.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 
The fleet services division of the department of executive services shall be
the executive agency in charge of implementing the provisions of this chapter. 
The division shall:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  Develop
the administrative rules to implement the provisions of this chapter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Evaluate
all take-home vehicle assignment requests from executive departments and
administrative offices; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  Approve
and monitor take-home vehicle assignments requested by executive departments
and administrative offices.  (Ord. 18791 § 16, 2018:  Ord. 14199 § 32, 2001: 
Ord. 12077 § 13, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.30.045  Commuting
in county-owned vehicle a taxable benefit.</span></b><span style='font-size:
12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Commuting in a
county-owned vehicle, whether assigned or occasional use, is a taxable benefit
for the employee.  (Ord. 19953 § 101, 2025).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.30.050 
Record-keeping.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The fleet services division of the department of executive
services shall develop and maintain central records of all county take-home
vehicle assignments.  The records shall be maintained in one location and shall
be readily available upon request.  At a minimum, the record-keeping should
contain:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  Vehicle
assignment by department, division, position title, and employee name;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Mileage
including a breakdown of commuting mileage and county business mileage based on
a trip log;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  Number and
nature of emergency response assignments, if the take-home vehicle is assigned
based on an emergency response justification; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  A
calculation of savings if take-home vehicle assignment is based on an economic
justification.  (Ord. 19953 § 102, 2025:  Ord. 18791 § 17, 2018:  Ord. 12077 §
14, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.30.060  Biannual
reevaluation and update of take-home vehicle assignments.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The
fleet services division of the department of executive services shall, semiannually,
reevaluate and update all executive department take-home vehicle assignments.  Biannually,
the fleet services division shall make available to the council and the public 
an updated  list of take-home vehicle assignments.  The updated list shall
identify each take-home vehicle assignment by department, division, and
position title.  In addition, there should be written documentation for each
take-home vehicle assignment which describes how each assignment meets the
policies and criteria set forth in this chapter.  (Ord. 19953 § 103, 2025:  Ord.
18791 § 18, 2018:  Ord. 12077 § 15, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.30.070 
Exemptions.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  Occasional
Overnight Usage.  Occasional overnight usage of county-owned vehicles is
permitted.  Occasional overnight usage may involve:</span></p>

<p class=MsoBodyText2><span style='font-size:12.0pt'>              1.  Taking a
county vehicle home before or after attending a meeting away from the employee's
workplace; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  Taking a
county vehicle home when an employee has primary responsibility to respond to
emergencies caused by inclement weather, such as, flooding or heavy snowstorms.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Collective
Bargaining Agreement.  The terms and conditions of a collective bargaining
agreement that provide for take home vehicle assignments shall supersede this
chapter for represented employees.  (Ord. 19953 § 104, 2025:  Ord. 11183 § 8,
1993:  Ord. 10930 § 11, 1993).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc94867925"></a><a name="_Toc151386334"><span style='font-size:
12.0pt'>3.32    COUNTY AUTOMOTIVE PARKING FACILITIES</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.006      Definitions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.010      Administrative regulations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.020      Parking fees - general - method
of payment - market survey.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.040      Public parking spaces.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.042      Parking rates - monthly.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.045      Goat Hill and King Street
Center parking garages – parking rates.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.050      Reserved spaces for disabled.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.060      Penalties.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.090      Goat Hill parking facility,
Chinook Building and King Street Center parking portion - parking rates.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.006 
Definitions.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The definitions in this section apply throughout this
chapter unless the context clearly requires otherwise.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  &quot;After-hours
parking&quot; means work related parking Monday through Friday for employees
whose normal work shifts begin after 1:30 p.m. and end between 9:00 p.m. and
5:00 a.m. or who are required to come into work after 4:30 p.m.  &quot;After-hours
parking&quot; also includes work-related parking on weekends and holidays that
is required by the employee's agency.  &quot;After-hours parking&quot; does not
include parking for non-related activities.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  &quot;Business
convenience&quot; means a county business-related requirement.  For the
purposes of this chapter, &quot;a county business-related requirement&quot;
includes after-hours parking for county employees, parking for commissioned
sheriff's office personnel, parking for county employees working for a
specified and limited period on a time-sensitive project that requires them to
arrive before or stay after regular work hours, parking for county employees
who are required as part of their jobs to use their private vehicles to
routinely travel to multiple county business locations, parking for
county-owned vehicles and paid parking for county volunteers authorized by
ordinance or by any presiding elected official as defined by K.C.C. 3.24.010 or
the presiding elected official's designee, but for the executive branch any
designee must be at least the highest-ranking employee of a division.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  &quot;County
automotive parking facility&quot; means:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  The Goat
Hill parking facility located at Sixth Avenue and Jefferson Street in Seattle;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  The Norm
Maleng Regional Justice Center parking facility located at 401 Fourth Avenue
North in Kent;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3. The King County
adult correctional facility parking facility located at Fifth Avenue and James Street
in Seattle;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  The
Chinook Building parking facility located at Fifth Avenue and Jefferson Street
in Seattle; </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              5.  The King
Street Center parking facility, located at 201 South Jackson Street in Seattle;
and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              6.  The
Judge Patricia H. Clark Children and Family Justice Center parking facility
located at 1211 East Alder Street in Seattle.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  &quot;County
volunteer&quot; means a person who is not a county employee, who performs
service for the county for civic, charitable or humanitarian reasons, without
promise, expectation or receipt of compensation from the county for services
rendered and who is accepted as a volunteer by the county.  &quot;County
volunteer&quot; includes, but is not limited to, a person serving as a board
member, officer, commission member, volunteer intern or direct service
volunteer.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  &quot;Director&quot;
means the director of the county department of executive services or designee,
unless otherwise specified in this chapter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F. 
&quot;Electric Vehicle&quot; means a vehicle that uses only electricity, must
be plugged in to charge and is propelled by one or more electric motors powered
by rechargeable battery packs.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            G. </span> <span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&quot;Employees
with disabilities&quot; means county employees with disabilities that make it
difficult or impossible to use public transit, and who display a disabled
parking permit.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            H. 
&quot;Motorcycle&quot; means a motor vehicle designed to travel on not more
than three wheels in contact with the ground, on which the driver rides astride
the motor unit or power train and is designed to be steered with a handle bar,
excluding a farm tractor, an electric personal assistive mobility device and a
moped.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            I.  &quot;Public
parking&quot; means general purpose parking by persons who are not county
employees.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            J.  &quot;Qualified
employee carpool&quot; means employees who have been qualified as part of a
carpool through the King County Employee Transportation Program.  (Ord. 19542 </span><span
lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 1, 2022:  Ord. 19243 </span><span lang=DE
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
1, 2021:  Ord. 18618 </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 94, 2017:  Ord. 18402 §
3, 2016:  Ord. 17035 § 1, 2011:  Ord. 16981 § 2, 2010:  Ord. 16677 § 1, 2009: 
Ord. 15648 § 2, 2006).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.010 
Administrative regulations.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Administrative regulations adopted
by the department of executive services shall include, but not be limited to,
the following subjects:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A. 
Identification of positions eligible for reserved and unreserved parking;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Regulation
of after-hours and weekend parking;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C. 
Enforcement procedures, including certification and monitoring of carpools and
the establishment of procedures for handling complaints;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D. 
Designation of the public parking areas;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E. 
Prohibition of the resale of the parking stall assignment;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F. 
Designation of reserved and unreserved parking areas; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            G. 
Establishment of public parking rates at the county automotive parking
facilities, excluding the King County International Airport which is governed
by K.C.C. Title 15.  The public parking rates shall be based upon the advice
and market rate information regularly collected and analyzed by the private
contractor hired to manage and operate the parking facility or by the director
in those cases where the parking facility is not managed by a private
contractor.  The director may authorize the contractor to make periodic rate
adjustments within a specified range, but shall periodically review the
contractor's rate adjustments, rate setting methods and research to ensure that
they are based on current data.  (Ord. 19542 </span><span lang=DE
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
2, 2022:  Ord. 19243 </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 2, 2021:  Ord. 15950 § 1,
2007:  Ord. 15648 § 3, 2006:  Ord. 14713 § 1, 2003:  Ord. 14262 § 3, 2001: 
Ord. 12077 § 16, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.020 
Parking fees - general - method of payment - market survey.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            </span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>A. 
All county employees, elected officials and county volunteers shall pay their
own parking fees unless they are parking for the business convenience of the
county.  The agency served by an employee, elected official or county
volunteer, who is parking for the business convenience of the county, shall be
charged for such parking at a rate equal to the county employee rate.  Current
county employees covered by existing collective bargaining agreements shall be
affected only in a manner consistent with state law.  There shall not be any
other exemptions from parking fees except as otherwise provided in a collective
bargaining agreement.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  The
parking fees for monthly parking that is not for the business convenience of
the county shall be paid through monthly payroll deductions from the wages of
the employee or elected official who is assigned the regular use of the county
parking stall regardless of whether that person is assigned the use of a
county-owned vehicle, assigned a vehicle provided at county expense or uses a
privately owned vehicle.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  In order
to evaluate the appropriateness of any potential adjustments to established
employee parking rates, the facilities management division of the department of
executive services may conduct a survey of the monthly parking rates charged
for public parking in comparable parking facilities.  The survey and any
employee parking fee recommendations shall be forwarded to the council for
consideration during the budget process.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  The public
parking rates shall be based upon the advice and market rate information
regularly collected and analyzed by the private contractor hired to manage and
operate the parking facility or by the director in those cases where the parking
facility is not managed by a private contractor.  The director may authorize
the contractor to make periodic rate adjustments within a specific range, but
shall periodically review the contractor's rate adjustments, rate setting
methods and research to ensure that they are based on current data.  (Ord.
19542 </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 3, 2022:  Ord. 19243 </span><span lang=DE
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
3, 2021:  Ord. 16981 § 2, 2010:  Ord. 15648 § 4, 2006:  Ord. 14713 § 2, 2003: 
Ord. 14262 § 4, 2001:  Ord. 12077 § 17, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.040 
Public parking space.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Public parking space within county automotive parking
facilities shall be made available to the extent practicable.  Members of the
public on county business shall be charged for all time parked.  Religious
service volunteers who work at the King County correctional facility are
parking for the business convenience of the department of adult and juvenile
detention.  (Ord. 19542 § 4, 2022:  Ord. 16981 § 3, 2010:  Ord. 10290 § 1,
1992:  Ord. 10175 § 3, 1991:  Ord. 8753 § 3, 1988:  Ord. 7863 § 2, 1986:  Ord.
3511 § 5, 1977).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.042 
Parking rates - monthly.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  Monthly
employee and county vehicle rates for parking in the parking facilities at Goat
Hill, the Chinook Building, the King County correctional facility and the King
Street Center shall be as follows:</span></p>

<table class=MsoNormalTable border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0
 style='margin-left:23.4pt;border-collapse:collapse'>
