 </tr>
</table>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Monthly
employee and county vehicle rates for parking in the Judge Patricia H. Clark
Children and Family Justice Center parking facility shall be ten dollars.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  Employees
with disabilities shall receive first priority in the assignment of available
parking stalls.  (Ord. 19542 </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
5, 2022:  Ord. 19243 </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 6, 2021:  Ord. 18402 § 9,
2016:  Ord. 18402 § 6, 2016:  Ord. 16981 § 5, 2010:  Ord. 16696 § 2, 2009: 
Ord. 16367 § 2, 2008:  15950 § 5, 2007:  Ord. 15648 § 9, 2006:  Ord. 14713 § 4,
2003:  Ord. 14262 § 6, 2001:  Ord. 13337 § 4, 1998:  Ord. 12077 § 18, 1995. 
Formerly K.C.C. 3.32.055).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.045  Goat
Hill and King Street Center parking garages - parking rates.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The
rates for county employees parking at the Goat Hill and King Street Center parking
facilities shall be as follows:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  Daily
parking for motorcycles:  five dollars;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  Hourly
parking:  Hourly public parking rates shall apply up to a maximum of twenty
dollars per day for automobiles; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  The rate
for after-hours work-related parking shall be seven dollars per day.  (Ord.
19542 </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 6, 2022:  Ord. 19243 </span><span lang=DE
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
7, 2021:  Ord. 18402 § 8, 2016:  Ord. 18402 § 5, 2016:  Ord. 16981 § 4, 2010: 
Ord. 16367 § 1, 2009:  Ord. 15950 § 3, 2007:  Ord. 15648 § 7, 2006:  Ord. 14713
§ 3, 2003:  Ord. 14262 § 5, 2001:  Ord. 13337 § 2, 1998:  Ord. 11133 § 2,
1993:  Ord. 10175 § 4, 1991:  Ord. 9413 § 3, 1990:  Ord. 8816 § 2, 1989:  Ord.
8753 § 6, 1988).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.050 
Parking assignments.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  Specifically designated parking stalls shall be
reserved on a first-come, first-serve basis for persons with disabilities.</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 
(Ord. 19243 </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 8, 2021:  Ord. 15648 § 8, 2006:  Ord. 3511 § 6, 1977).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.060 
Penalties.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The director of the department of executive services is
authorized to issue overtime parking citations and to impound vehicles found to
be in violation of any of the provisions of this chapter or the regulations
promulgated under this chapter.  The director may rescind the parking privilege
of persons found to be in violation of this chapter or the regulations
promulgated under this chapter.  (Ord. 14262 § 7, 2001:  Ord. 12077 § 19,
1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.32.090  Goat
Hill parking facility, Chinook Building and King Street Center parking portion
- parking rates.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  All revenues derived from parking fees shall be
distributed as follows:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  Applicable
local and state taxes;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  An
allocation to cover rent payments or debt service payments for the Goat Hill
parking facility and prorated rent for the parking portion of the Chinook
Building and King Street Center;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  It is the
intent of the council to, during the budget process, identify a portion of the
revenue going to the current expense fund to be transferred to the major
maintenance reserve fund to support major maintenance projects at the parking
facilities, taking into account the major maintenance model and financial plan;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  An
allocation to the facilities management division of the department of executive
service’s internal service fund to reimburse the fund for expenses associated
with the operation of the parking facilities; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  The
residual revenues distributed to the general fund.  (Ord. 19654 § 8, 2023:  Ord.
19542 § 7, 2022:  Ord. 19243 </span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>
10, 2021:  Ord. 17926 § 4, 2014:  Ord. 15950 § 6, 2007:  Ord. 15648 § 10,
2006:  Ord. 14639 § 1, 2003:  Ord. 14262 § 8, 2001:  Ord. 10290 § 3, 1992: 
Ord. 10175 § 6, 1991:  Ord. 8753 § 7, 1988).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc94867926"></a><a name="_Toc151386335"><span style='font-size:
12.0pt'>3.36    CHARITABLE CONTRIBUTIONS FROM COUNTY EMPLOYEES</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.36.010      Intent and purpose.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.36.020      Definitions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.36.030      Employee giving program
committee.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.36.035      Program administrator.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.36.045      Program participants -
requirements - notice in program materials.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.36.055      Solicitations of donations -
limits on use of county property or equipment – promotional costs - voluntary
employee participation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.36.065      Deductions from salary warrants
for donations - one-time donations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.36.075      Disbursements for
contributions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.36.010 
Intent and purpose.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  This
chapter is intended to establish uniform guidance, consistent with state law
governing salary and wage deductions, for the efficient administration of
county employee charitable contributions and volunteering to qualified
nonprofit organizations, donated via the annual drive, emergency or disaster
relief solicitations, and other charitable solicitations.  This chapter shall
be liberally construed to accomplish this intention.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  The
purpose of this chapter is to provide a convenient and effective channel
through which county employees may contribute to qualified nonprofit
organizations, while minimizing disruption to the county workplace and the
costs to the taxpayer that multiple charitable fund drives cause; and to
enhance government and community efforts to meet charitable needs.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-indent:.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>C. 
The program shall provide guidance, quality control, and disbursement of
employee donations to qualified nonprofit organizations and federations as
provided by this chapter, in accordance with rules for the program.  (Ord.
19953 § 105, 2025:  Ord. 18329 § 1, 2016:  Ord. 17332 § 1, 2012:  Ord. 16035 §
1, 2008:  Ord. 8575 § 1, 1988).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.36.020 
Definitions.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>  The definitions in this section apply throughout this
chapter unless the context clearly requires otherwise.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  &quot;Annual
drive&quot; means the annual solicitation of contributions from county
employees by representatives of qualified nonprofit organizations and
federations through oral presentations, printed materials, audio or video media,
or other similar means.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  &quot;Committee&quot;
means the county employee giving program committee established under K.C.C.
3.36.030.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C. 
&quot;Emergency or disaster relief solicitation&quot; means the opportunity to
donate, in accordance with K.C.C. 3.12.222, in response to the occurrence of an
emergency or disaster, such as fire, flood, explosion, storm, earthquake, or
epidemic, that results in the loss of either life or property, or both.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            D.  &quot;Employee
giving program&quot; or &quot;the program&quot; means the year-round King
County sanctioned, employee-based program that provides the process and
infrastructure for  administration of employee-directed giving and volunteering
to qualified nonprofit organizations and federations and is administered by the
committee in accordance with this chapter and any rules adopted for the
program.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            E.  &quot;Federation&quot;
means a nonprofit organization that solicits and distributes contributions on
behalf of its member nonprofit organizations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            F.  &quot;Qualified
nonprofit organization&quot; means a nonprofit organization or federation that
applies to participate in the program and meets the eligibility criteria as
provided in this chapter and any rules adopted for the program.  (Ord. 19953 § 106,
2025:  Ord. 18329 § 2, 2016:  Ord. 17332 § 2, 2012:  Ord. 16035 § 2, 2008: Ord.
9091, 1989:  Ord. 8575 § 2, 1988).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.36.030 
Employee giving program committee.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            A.  A county
employee giving program committee is established consisting of fifteen members
nominated by the committee, appointed by the executive, and confirmed by the
council.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  The
committee shall strive in its nominations to include members representing the
diversity of the county work force, including union representation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2.  The term
of committee members shall be two years.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3.  A
committee member who serves as a federation or nonprofit organization board
member or director, or in a decision-making capacity for a federation or
nonprofit organization, shall not vote on that federation or nonprofit
organization's eligibility if that federation or nonprofit organization applies
to participate in the program.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  The
committee shall annually elect a chair and other officers as established in the
committee's bylaws.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            B.  In order
to operate the program, the committee may:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              1.  Adopt
rules and bylaws consistent with this chapter that are necessary to the conduct
of the program, based upon the following principles:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                a.  seek
operational efficiencies;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                b. 
enhance program effectiveness;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                c.  use
innovative best practices;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                d. 
promote equitable access for nonprofit participation; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>                e. 
maintain standards to ensure nonprofit fiscal responsibility and stability;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              2. 
Establish and apply eligibility rules by which a nonprofit organization may
participate in the program;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              3 
Coordinate and facilitate the program consistent with this chapter and any
rules adopted for the program.  If the committee determines that a federation
or nonprofit organization is not eligible to participate in the program, the
federation or nonprofit organization may apply to the committee for
reconsideration of the eligibility decision;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              4.  Guide
fiscal stewardship of the program;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              5.  Assist
the executive or the executive's designee in the selection of a program
administrator; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>              6.  Solicit
and accept from the general public and business communities and all other
persons, gifts, bequests, and donations to the county in support of the
program.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            C.  Committee
members serve voluntarily and with the approval of their employing department. 
Committee members shall be given release time from regular work hours to serve
on the committee and shall be reimbursed by their employing department for any
applicable travel, lodging, and meals in accordance with county laws and
regulations.  Employees covered by the overtime requirements of the Fair Labor
Standards Act or state law who volunteer to serve as committee members shall
track and submit all hours worked for the committee, and shall ensure that
their working hours, including hours worked for the committee, are approved by
their supervisors in advance.  (Ord. 19953 § 107, 2025:  Ord. 18329 § 3, 2016: 
Ord. 17527 § 127, 2013:  Ord. 17332 § 3, 2012:  Ord. 16035 § 3, 2008:  Ord.
11997, 1995:  Ord. 10923 § 1, 1993:  Ord. 9465 § 1, 1990:  Ord. 8575 § 3,
1988).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>3.36.035 
Program administrator.</b>  The program administrator shall be responsible for
the operational details of the program, including the annual drive and emergency
or disaster relief solicitations, under the general oversight of the
committee.  The cost of the program administrator shall be included as part of
the administrative cost of the program.  (Ord. 19953 § 108, 2025:  Ord. 17332 §
4, 2012).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.36.045  Program participants -
requirements - notice in program materials.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  A federation or nonprofit
organization may participate in the program if the federation or nonprofit
organization submits a timely application for participation to the committee
and meets all eligibility requirements as established by this chapter and any
rules adopted for the program.  An official of the federation or nonprofit
organization must certify on the program application that the federation, each
nonprofit organization represented by the federation, or the nonprofit
organization:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  Is formally recognized by the
United States Internal Revenue Service as complying with Section 501(c)(3) of
the Internal Revenue Code of 1986 or is a governmental unit of the state of
Washington, and for which all contributions to the nonprofit organization are
eligible to be deductible for federal income tax purposes under Section 170 of
the Internal Revenue Code of 1986;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Is registered with the
Washington state Secretary of State as provided by RCW 19.09.065 and is in
compliance with Washington state laws governing charities to the best of the
knowledge of the individual certifying the application;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.a.  Does not discriminate
against any person on the basis of race, color, religious affiliation, sex,
age, national origin, marital status, sexual orientation, disability, or gender
identity or expression or qualifies for an exemption under Title VII of the
Civil Rights Act of 1964 as amended.  An affirmation of a participating
organization's adherence to this subsection A.3.a, or a statement of exemption
from this subsection A.3.a, must be included in the organization's
application.  A federation must affirm in the federation's application the
adherence to this subsection A.3.a, or a legal exception from this subsection
A.3.a, for each nonprofit organization the federation represents.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                b.  Nothing in this subsection
A.3. denies eligibility to a federation or nonprofit organization that is
otherwise eligible to participate in the program merely because the federation
or nonprofit organization is organized by, on behalf of or to serve persons of
a particular race, color, religious affiliation, sex, national origin, age,
marital status, sexual orientation, disability, or gender identity or
expression.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-autospace:none'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B. 
Participating organizations' responses provided under subsection A. of this
section may be noted in program materials.  (Ord. 19953 § 109, 2025:  Ord.
17332 § 6, 2012:  Ord. 17047 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span>1, 2011
(expired January 1, 2012):  Ord. 16035 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ 5</span>,
2008).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.36.055  Solicitations of
donations - limits on use of county property or equipment - promotional costs -
voluntary employee participation.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  Employees may be solicited for
program contributions in accordance with this chapter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  Solicitations and events
related to the program must be conducted on county property or online using
county resources during normal county business hours.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.  Employees may use county
property for the purposes of solicitations for the promotion of the program.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            D.  A county employee shall not be
coerced or required to participate in any program activities or to make any
donation to a qualified nonprofit organization.  A county employee shall not be
penalized for failing to participate in the program.  Departments and offices
may authorize time for department employees to attend program-related
activities.  (Ord. 19953 § 110, 2025:  Ord. 17332 § 7, 2012:  Ord. 16035 <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§ 6</span>, 2008).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.36.065 Deductions from salary
warrants for donations - one-time donations.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  Donations under this chapter
may include payroll deductions, checks, money orders, cash, electronic payments,
and time donations in accordance with K.C.C. 3.12.222.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  The county shall make
deductions from county employees' salary warrants and pay the moneys collected
to the qualified nonprofit organizations and federations designated by county
employees when the deductions and payments are authorized by county employees
in accordance with this chapter.  (Ord. 19953 § 111, 2025:  <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 18329 § 4, 2016:  </span>Ord. 17332 § 8,
2012:  Ord. 16035 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span> 7, 2008)</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.36.075  Disbursements of
contributions.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.<b>  </b>After program costs have
been paid, all payroll deductions must be fully disbursed by the county to the
designated qualified nonprofit organizations by the end of the first quarter
following the deduction year.  Federations shall make distributions to their
member charitable organizations as designated by contributors.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  Any undesignated contributions
shall be distributed proportionately to the participating organizations.  (Ord.
19953 § 112, 2025:  Ord. 17332 § 9, 2012:  Ord. 16035 <span style='letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>§</span> 8, 2008).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc94867927"></a><a name="_Toc151386336"><span style='font-size:
12.0pt'>3.39    SEATTLE METROPOLITAN POLICE MUSEUM CONTRIBUTIONS FROM COUNTY</span>
EMPLOYEES</a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Sections:</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.39.010      Purpose.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.39.020      Deductions -- participation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>3.39.010 
Purpose</b>.  The purpose of this chapter is to provide a convenient channel
through which county employees may contribute membership fees to the Seattle
Metropolitan Police Museum.  (Ord. 13909 § 1, 2000).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            <b>3.39.020 
Deductions -- participation.</b>  The county shall make deductions from a
county employee's salary warrants and pay the moneys collected to the Seattle
Metropolitan Police Museum if the employee authorizes the deductions and
payments.  A county employee's participation in the program is strictly
voluntary.  The coercion of an employee to participate or to make a donation to
the program, and the penalization of an employee for failing to participate in
the program, are prohibited.  (Ord. 13909 § 2, 2000).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc94867928"></a><a name="_Toc151386337"><span style='font-size:
12.0pt'>3.42    WHISTLEBLOWER PROTECTION</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Sections</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.42.010      Policy - purpose.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.42.020      Definitions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.42.030      Right to report - procedures.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.42.040      Confidentiality.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.42.050      Investigations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.42.055      Investigations by investigating
official - powers - procedures - prosecutor powers.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.42.057      Investigation by the office of
public complaints - powers - procedures - fines.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='margin-left:99.0pt;text-align:justify;text-indent:
-99.0pt'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>            3.42.060      Reporting and adjudicating
retaliation - directors of divisions of the office of public complaints,
prosecutor powers and procedures.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.42.010  Policy - purpose.</b> 
Unless prohibited by state law, county employees are encouraged to report on
improper governmental action to the appropriate county or other government
official.  To assist such reporting and to implement chapter 42.41 RCW, this
chapter provides county employees a process for reporting improper governmental
action and protection from retaliatory action for reporting and cooperating in
the investigation and/or prosecution of improper governmental action in good
faith in accordance with this chapter.  (Ord. 16580 <span style='letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>§</span> 1, 2009:  Ord. 11687 § 3, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.42.020  Definitions.</b>  The
definitions in this section apply throughout this chapter unless the context
clearly requires otherwise.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  &quot;Appropriate investigating
official&quot; means an investigating official acting within the investigating
official's respective jurisdiction as identified in K.C.C. 3.42.030.D. or any
assistant or representative authorized to receive documents on the
investigating official's behalf, except that for the department of public
safety, the only appropriate investigating official shall be the internal
investigations unit or any assistant or representative authorized to receive
documents on its behalf.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  &quot;Employee&quot; or &quot;county
employee&quot; means any individual who is appointed as an employee by the
appointing authority of a county agency, office, department, council, board,
commission or other separate unit or division of county government, however
designated.  &quot;Employee&quot; or &quot;county employee&quot; also includes
county elected officials and members of county boards, commissions, committees,
or other multi-member bodies.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.  &quot;Good faith&quot; means
the individual providing the information or report of improper governmental
action has a reasonable basis in fact for reporting or providing the
information. An individual who knowingly provides or reports, or who reasonably
ought to know that the information or report is malicious, false or frivolous,
or information that is provided with reckless disregard for the truth, is not
acting in good faith.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            D.  &quot;Gross mismanagement&quot;
means the exercise of management responsibilities in a manner grossly deviating
from the standard of care or competence that a reasonable person would observe
in the same situation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            E.1.  &quot;Improper governmental
action&quot; means any action by a county officer or employee that is
undertaken in the performance of the officer's or employee's official duties,
whether or not the action is within the scope of employment, and:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                a.  violates any state or
federal law or rule or county ordinance or rule;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                b.  constitutes an abuse of
authority;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                c.  is gross mismanagement;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                d.  creates a substantial and
specific danger to the public health or safety;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                e.  results in a waste of
public moneys;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                f. results in abuse of public
moneys;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                g. results in fraud; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                h.  prevents the dissemination
of scientific opinion or alters technical findings without scientifically valid
justification, unless disclosure is legally prohibited.  This subsection E.1.h.
is not meant to preclude the discretion of agency management to adopt a
particular scientific opinion or technical finding from among differing
opinions or technical findings to the exclusion of other scientific opinions or
technical findings.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  &quot;Improper governmental
action&quot; does not include violations of anti-discrimination laws,
violations of collective bargaining or civil service laws, or alleged
violations of agreements with labor organizations under collective bargaining. 
A properly authorized county program or activity does not become an &quot;improper
governmental action&quot; because an employee or investigating official
dissents from the county policy or considers the expenditures unwise.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            F.  &quot;Investigating official&quot;
means any individual to whom a report may be made pursuant to K.C.C.
3.42.030.D. or any assistant or representative authorized to receive documents
on the investigating official's behalf.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            G.  &quot;Retaliate,&quot; &quot;retaliation&quot;
and &quot;retaliatory action,&quot; means to make any unwarranted adverse
change in an employee's employment status or the terms and conditions of
employment including, but not limited to:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  Denial of adequate staff to
perform duties;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Frequent staff changes;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  Frequent and undesirable
office changes;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              4.  Refusal to assign meaningful
work;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              5.  Unwarranted and
unsubstantiated letters of reprimand or unsatisfactory performance evaluations;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              6.  Demotion;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              7.  Reduction in pay;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              8.  Denial of promotion;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              9.  Denial of training or
benefits;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              10.  Transfer or reassignment;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              11.  Suspension or dismissal;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              12.  Other unwarranted
disciplinary action;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              13.  A supervisor or senior
manager or official behaving in or encouraging coworkers to behave in a hostile
manner toward the employee, or failing to take appropriate action to prevent
coworkers from behaving in a hostile manner toward the employee.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            H.  &quot;Substantial and specific
danger&quot; means a risk of serious injury, illness, peril or loss, to which
the exposure of the public is a gross deviation from the standard of care or
competence which a reasonable person would observe in the same situation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            I.  &quot;Waste&quot; means the
thoughtless or careless expenditure, mismanagement, or abuse of resources to
the detriment, or potential detriment of the county.  Examples of detriment to
the county include significant unnecessary costs, inefficiencies, and
ineffective practices, systems, or controls.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            J.  &quot;Written report of
improper governmental action&quot; means any writing that alleges that an
improper governmental action has occurred and describes the basis for that
belief.  (Ord. 20101 § 33, 2026:  <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 18618
</span></span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 95, 2017:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 16580 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span>
2, 2009:  Ord. 11687 § 2, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.42.030  Right to report -
procedures</b>.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  Every county employee shall
have the right to report, in good faith in accordance with Ordinance 11687,
information concerning an improper governmental action.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  In reporting improper
governmental action, the employee is encouraged, but not required, to make a
written report first to any investigating official as defined by K.C.C.
3.42.020.G; the employee is encouraged to consult with the office of public
complaints in order to determine to whom a written report should be made.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.1.  This section does not
authorize a county employee to report information that is subject to an
applicable privilege against disclosure at law (e.g., RCW 5.60.060 privileged
communications) unless waived, or to make disclosure where prohibited at law.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  An employee making a written
report under this subsection is encouraged to wait at least thirty days from
receipt of the written report by the investigating official before reporting
the improper governmental action to a person who is not an investigating
official.  However, reporting to a person who is not an investigating official
before this thirty-day period will not result in the loss of the protections in
this chapter.  </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  An employee's reporting of
the employee's own improper action does not grant the employee immunity from
discipline or termination insofar as the employee's improper action would be
cause for discipline.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            D.  For purposes of this chapter,
the person to whom a written report should be made is as follows:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  Reporting sexual harassment
to the employee's supervisor, department head or other government official as
set out in the county's adopted procedure for reporting sexual harassment
complaints;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Reporting violations of the
fair employment practices ordinance, which is K.C.C. chapter 12.18, to the
executive or the executive's designee;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  Reporting police misconduct
to the department of public safety's internal investigation unit or to the
office of law enforcement oversight;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              4.  Reporting violations of the
Code of Judicial Conduct to the Washington state Commission on Judicial
Conduct;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              5.  Reporting improper
governmental action occurring within the district court to the presiding judge
of the district court;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              6.  Reporting improper
governmental action occurring within the legislative branch to the chair of the
council or to the prosecutor;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              7.  Reporting improper
governmental action occurring within the executive branch to the executive or
to the department director of the executive agency in which the alleged
improper governmental action occurred or to the office of public complaints;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              8.  Reporting improper
governmental action occurring within the department of judicial administration
to the director/clerk of the superior court or to the office of public
complaints;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              9.  Reporting improper
governmental action occurring within the department of assessments to the
assessor or to the office of public complaints;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              10.  Reporting improper
governmental action occurring within the department of elections to the
director of elections or to the office of public complaints;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              11.  Reporting improper
governmental action occurring within the superior court to the presiding judge
of the superior court;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              12.  Reporting violations of
criminal laws to the sheriff or the county prosecuting attorney;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              13.  Reporting improper
governmental action of the county prosecuting attorney to the state auditor or
the attorney general;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              14.  Reporting improper
governmental action occurring within the office of economic and financial
analysis to any member of the forecast council or to the office of public
complaints;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              15.  Reporting violations of
K.C.C. chapter 3.04, the Employee Code of Ethics, to the office of public
complaints; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              16.  Reporting any improper
governmental action for which no other appropriate recipient of a report is
listed in subsection D.1. through 15. of this section to the office of public
complaints.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            E.  Any one or more of the
following conduct by employees is protected under this chapter:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  Reporting improper
governmental action;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Cooperating in an
investigation by any official related to improper governmental action,
including but not limited to local, state, federal, and internal investigation;
and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  Testifying in any official
proceeding, hearing, or prosecution arising out of an improper governmental
action.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            F.  A county officer or employee
shall not retaliate, attempt to retaliate or threaten to retaliate against any
employee because that employee has in good faith engaged in conduct protected
by K.C.C. 3.42.030.E., or because the county officer or employee believes the
employee has engaged or will engage in such conduct, whether or not such
conduct actually occurred.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            G.  Any county officer or employee
who engages in retaliatory action prohibited by K.C.C. 3.42.030.F. is subject
to disciplinary action including, but not limited to, suspension without pay,
demotion or termination.  In addition, any elected official who engages in
retaliatory action prohibited by K.C.C. 3.42.030.F. is subject to censure by
motion of the council and also may be subject to recall from office due to
misfeasance or malfeasance in office.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            H.  Each appointing authority shall
ensure that, upon entering county service or any time there are material
changes to this chapter, every county officer and employee shall receive a
written summary of this chapter, the procedures for reporting improper
governmental actions to investigating officials, the procedures for obtaining
the protections extended, the prohibition against retaliation in this section,
and identification of offices and resources available to help the employee
understand the provisions of this chapter including but not limited to, the
office of public complaints.  The office of public complaints shall assist in
the development of materials.  Copies of these summaries shall be conspicuously
posted where all employees will have reasonable access to them.  Every county
officer and employee shall also receive a written summary of this chapter at least
once every two years; the summary may be distributed electronically.  (Ord.
20101 § 34, 2026:  <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 18618 </span></span><span
lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 96, 2017:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 16580 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span>
3, 2009:  Ord. 11687 § 4, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.42.040  Confidentiality</b>. 
To the extent allowed by the Public Disclosure Act, RCW 42.56.240 and other
laws, the identity or identifying characteristics the identity of an employee
reporting information about an improper governmental action or cooperating in
an investigation of improper governmental action under K.C.C. 3.42.030E.1. or
K.C.C. 3.42.030E.2. shall be kept confidential from all persons except for
investigating officials and their staff.  However, the employee may waive
confidentiality in a written waiver or by making the employee's own identity
known in connection with the protected conduct in the course of public
testimony or by acknowledging the employee's own identity in a claim against
the county for retaliation.  If applicable, the complainant may state in
writing whether the complainant wishes the complainant's own name not to be
disclosed pursuant to the provisions of RCW 42.56.240(2), which exempts
information revealing the identity of persons who are witnesses to or victims
of crime or who file complaints with investigative, law enforcement or penology
agencies, other than the commission, if disclosure would endanger any person's
life, physical safety or property.  (<span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord.
18618 </span></span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 97, 2017:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 16580 § 4, 2009:  Ord. 11687 § 5, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.42.050  Investigations</b>.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  If the official receiving a
complaint under this section is not the appropriate investigating official
identified in K.C.C. 3.42.030.D.1, the official receiving the complaint shall
immediately forward the written report to the appropriate investigating
official and notify the reporting employee of the referral.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  If a report of improper
governmental action meets the definition of a complaint under K.C.C. 3.04.055,
the office of public complaints, upon receipt of the report, shall investigate
that allegation according to the procedures in K.C.C. chapter 3.04, the
Employee Code of Ethics.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.  If the office of public
complaints is an appropriate investigating official but the report does not
meet the definition of a complaint under K.C.C. chapter 3.04, the Employee Code
of Ethics, the office of public complaints upon receipt of the report may refer
the report to the department director of the agency in which the alleged
improper governmental action occurred or to the chief elected official of the
branch of government implicated in the allegation; if the office of public
complaints does not refer to another official, or if the other official's
response is not timely or satisfactory to the ombuds, the office of public
complaints shall conduct an investigation in accordance with the procedures
outlined in K.C.C. 3.42.057.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            D.  If a report of improper
governmental action is filed with an appropriate investigating official who is
not the office of public complaints, and a report is concurrently filed with
the office of public complaints, the office of public complaints may defer
action until the investigation is completed by the affected department, office
or agency.  When the office of public complaints chooses to conduct a
concurrent investigation the office of public complaints shall notify the
executive and the chair of the council.  </span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            E.  Decisions of the office of
public complaints under this section may not be appealed to the Board of
Ethics.  (Ord. 20101 § 35, 2026:  <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 18618
</span></span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 98, 2017:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 16580 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span>
5, 2009:  Ord. 11687 § 6, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.42.055  Investigations by
investigating officials - powers - procedures - prosecutor powers.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  The procedures in this section
shall apply to any investigating official except those within the office of
public complaints or the judicial branch. Investigations by the office of
public complaints shall be conducted in accordance with K.C.C. 3.42.057.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  When an appropriate
investigating official who is not within the office of public complaints
receives a report of improper governmental action, the investigating official
shall respond to the reporting employee in writing within thirty days of when
the report was received with either a final report or a preliminary report,
with a copy of the response to the office of public complaints.  If responding
with a preliminary report, the official shall include a summary of the status
of the investigation and information obtained thus far, and identifying matters
for further research or inquiry.  If the identity of the reporting employee is
not known, the response shall be sent to the office of public complaints.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.  The investigating official
shall complete the investigation and issue a final report no later than one
year from when the report of improper governmental action was received.  If the
final report concludes that there was improper governmental action, it shall
include an action plan for addressing the improper governmental action and
provide reasonable timelines for completing corrective actions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            D.  The investigating official
shall send a copy of the final report to the reporting employee and the office
of public complaints.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            E.  When conducting an
investigation of improper governmental action occurring within the legislative
branch, the prosecutor may at any stage, issue subpoenas, administer oaths,
examine witnesses, and compel the production of documents or other evidence;
refer the matter to the state auditor, law enforcement authorities or other
governmental agency; and issue reports; or any combination thereof, each as
deemed appropriate.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            F.  If the investigating official
determines that that the employee reporting improper governmental action has
been retaliated against or is at great risk of retaliation, the investigating
official may seek temporary preventive action, including but not limited to the
transfer of the reporting employee to another department at the request of the
reporting employee or authorizing leave with pay for the reporting employee. If
the investigating official deems it necessary, the investigating official's
recommendation may be made to the executive<span class=MsoCommentReference>.</span> 
Such a temporary preventative action may continue until the conclusion of any
investigation and a permanent resolution of the matter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            G.  To the extent allowed by law,
investigating officials are encouraged to enter into cooperative agreements or
arrangements for receiving and processing complaints with other agencies or
entities that are investigating related complaints, so that duplication of
functions shall be minimized and multiple redundant investigations avoided.  (Ord.
20101 § 36, 2026:  <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 18728 § 95, 2018: 
Ord. 18618 </span></span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:
"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 99, 2017:  </span><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 16580 <span
style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span> 6, 2009).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.42.057  Investigation by the
office of public complaints - powers - procedures - fines.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  The procedures in this section
apply to the office of public complaints when a division within the office of
public complaints is investigating a report of an improper governmental action
that is not investigated according to the rules applicable to K.C.C. chapter
3.04, the Employee Code of Ethics.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  In determining whether to
conduct an investigation, the director of the applicable division within the
office of public complaints may consider factors including, but not limited to,
the nature and quality of the evidence and the existence of relevant laws and
rules; whether the alleged improper governmental action was isolated or
systematic; the history of previous assertions regarding the same subject or
subject matter; whether other avenues are available for addressing the matter;
whether the matter has already been investigated or is in litigation; the
seriousness or significance of the asserted improper governmental action; and the
cost and benefit of the investigation.  The director of the applicable division
has the sole discretion to determine the priority and weight given to these or
any other relevant factors and to decide whether a matter is to be
investigated.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.  If the director of the
applicable division elects not to investigate the matter, the director of the
applicable division shall, before making a final decision to close the
investigation, send a notice to the person who made the report explaining the
factors considered and the analysis applied, summarizing allegation
deficiencies if any, and providing a reasonable opportunity to reply.  The
notification may be by electronic means.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            D.  If the director of the
applicable division with the office of public complaints determines that that
the employee reporting improper governmental action has been retaliated against
or is at great risk of retaliation, the director of the applicable division may
recommend to the head of the department that temporary preventive action be
taken, including but not limited to transferring the reporting employee at the
reporting employee's request to another department or authorizing leave with
pay for the reporting employee.  If the director of the applicable division
deems it necessary, the director of the applicable division may make the
recommendation to the executive instead.  Such temporary preventative action
may continue until the conclusion of any investigation and a permanent
resolution of the matter.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            E.  If the director of the
applicable division within the office of public complaints  elects to conduct
an investigation and it appears to the director of the applicable division that
the investigation will take longer than thirty days to complete, the director
of the applicable division shall, within thirty days after receiving the report
of alleged improper governmental action, provide the complainant with a
preliminary written report that summarizes the procedural status of the
investigation, the information obtained thus far, any preliminary findings as
the director of the applicable division deems appropriate, and identifying
matters for further research or inquiry.  The director of the applicable
division shall also notify the subject or subjects of the investigation and the
agency head of the need for continued investigation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            F.  When conducting an
investigation, the director of the applicable division within the office of
public complaints  may at any stage issue subpoenas, administer oaths, examine
witnesses, and compel the production of documents or other evidence; refer the
matter to the state auditor, law enforcement authorities or other governmental
agency; and issue reports; or any combination thereof, each as deemed
appropriate.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            G.1.  Upon completion of an
investigation, the director of the applicable division within the office of
public complaints shall make a final written report that summarizes the results
of the investigation, including findings with regard to each assertion of
improper governmental action and recommended actions.  The director of the
applicable division shall complete the investigation and issue a final report
within one year of receipt of the report of improper governmental action.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  If the director of the
applicable division determines that no improper governmental action has
occurred, the director of the applicable division shall send the report to the
complainant, the subject or subjects of the investigation and the agency head.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  If the director of the
applicable division determines that an improper governmental action has
occurred:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                a.  The director of the
applicable division shall give the subject of the report an opportunity to
respond before issuing a final report;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                b.  The director of the
applicable division shall send the report to:  the complainant; the head of the
department with responsibility for the action or if a department head is
implicated, to the executive and county council; and such other governmental
officials or agencies as the director of the applicable division deems
appropriate.  The director of the applicable division shall also send a copy of
the written report to the executive or the county council if requested to do so
by the complainant, if the director of the applicable division has not already
done so;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                c.  The department with
responsibility for the improper governmental action shall report back to the director
of the applicable division and complainant with an action plan for addressing
the improper governmental action and provide reasonable timelines for
completing its corrective actions.  The department's response should be made
within fourteen days of receipt of the director of the applicable division 's
report.  If the director of the applicable division deems that satisfactory
action within a reasonable timeframe has not been achieved, the director of the
applicable division shall report the director of the applicable division's
determination to the executive and the county council; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                d.  The office of public
complaints may impose a fine of not greater than ten thousand dollars on the
department within which the improper governmental action occurred.  A fine
should be imposed for improper governmental actions that are exceptionally
egregious or for which corrective actions have been highly unsatisfactory.  The
department shall be given a reasonable opportunity to be heard before
imposition of any fine.  Proceeds collected from any fine shall be deposited
into an account to be used for the purpose of educating employees about this
chapter or may be applied by the department toward the cost of administrative
leave paid to the employee reporting the improper governmental action where the
reason for the administrative leave is related to the employee's reporting.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            H.1.  At any stage in the
investigation, the director of the applicable division may, with the agreement
of the parties, recommend, arrange for, convene, or conduct voluntary mediation
between the employee and either the subject of the investigation or agency
head, or both, with cost sharing, if any, to be determined by the parties.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  If the parties reach
agreement as a result of mediation, the director of the applicable division may
close the investigation.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  The response times from
subsection E. of this section shall be tolled for the duration of the mediation
process.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              4.  Mediation and other informal
resolution processes are voluntary. No employer or employee shall be pressured
into participating in such processes, and no negative inferences shall be drawn
if any party declines to participate in such processes.  If a party agrees to
participate in voluntary mediation or other informal resolution process, that
party is under no obligation to accept the resolution recommended by the
mediator, the director of the applicable division, or any other person
participating in this process, and no negative inferences shall be drawn as a
result of a refusal to accept such recommendations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            I.  The director of the applicable
division may close an investigation at any time the director of the applicable
division determines that no further action is warranted and shall so notify the
complainant, the subject or subjects of the investigation and the agency head.  The
director of the applicable division shall also issue any reports as required by
this section.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            J.  Decisions of the director of
the applicable division under this section may not be appealed to the board of
ethics.  (Ord. 20101 § 37, 2026:  <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 18618
</span></span><span lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 100, 2017:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 16580 <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>§</span>
7, 2009).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            <b>3.42.060  Reporting and
adjudicating retaliation - directors of divisions of the office of public
complaints, prosecutor powers and procedures.</b></span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify;text-indent:.5in'><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>A.  In order to seek
relief, an employee who believes the employee has been retaliated against in
violation of K.C.C. 3.42.030.E. must file a signed written complaint within six
months of when the alleged retaliation occurred or the employee reasonably
should have known of the occurrence.  The complaint shall be filed with the office
of public complaints and must specify the alleged retaliatory action and the
relief requested.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            B.  The director of the applicable
division within the office of public complaints shall conduct an investigation
of the alleged retaliatory action except that complaints involving the judicial
branch shall be forwarded to the appropriate investigating official for that
branch for investigation and complaints involving councilmembers shall be
forwarded to and investigated by the prosecutor.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            C.  When conducting an
investigation, the director of the applicable division within the office of
public complaints or prosecutor may at any stage, issue subpoenas, administer
oaths, examine witnesses, and compel the production of documents or other
evidence; refer the matter to the state auditor, law enforcement authorities or
other governmental agency; and issue reports; or any combination thereof, each
as deemed appropriate.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            D.  If it appears to the director
of the applicable division within the office of public complaints or prosecutor
after conducting an investigation that no retaliation has occurred, the director
of the applicable division or prosecutor shall so notify the complainant
summarizing the director of the applicable division's or prosecuter's findings
and providing a reasonable opportunity for the complainant to reply before
making a final determination.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            E.  The director of the applicable
division within the office of public complaints or prosecutor shall, within
forty-five days after receiving the report of alleged retaliatory action,
provide the complainant with a written report that summarizes the results of
the investigation, including findings with regard to each assertion of retaliation
and recommended actions.  The director of the applicable division or prosecutor
shall also send a copy of the written report to any governmental officials or
agencies as the director of the applicable division or prosecutor deems
appropriate.  If the director of the applicable division or prosecutor finds
that additional time is needed to complete the report, the director of the
applicable division or prosecutor shall notify the complainant in writing
before the expiration of the forty-five day response period, and shall specify
the reasons that additional time is required.  The effect of the notice is to
extend for forty-five days the time period in which a response must be made. 
Only two such extensions may be made.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            F.  The following apply to
investigations by the office of public complaints under this section:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  If it appears to the director
of the applicable division within the office of public complaints at any stage
in the process that the complainant is at great risk of retaliation, the director
of the applicable division may recommend to the head of the department that
temporary preventive action be taken, including but not limited to transferring
the individual to another department or authorizing leave with pay.  If the director
of the applicable division deems it necessary, the director of the applicable
division recommendation may be made to the executive instead.  Such temporary
preventative action may continue until the conclusion of any investigation and
a permanent resolution of the matter;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  If the director of the
applicable division determines that no retaliatory action has occurred, the director
of the applicable division shall send the report to the complainant, the
subject or subjects of the investigation and the agency head; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  If the director of the
applicable division determines that retaliatory action has occurred:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                a.  The director of the
applicable division shall give the subject of the investigation an opportunity
to respond before issuing a final report;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                b.  The director of the
applicable division shall send the report to:  the complainant; the head of the
department with responsibility for the action or if a department head is
implicated, to the executive and county council, and to such other governmental
officials or agencies as the director of the applicable division deems
appropriate.  The director of the applicable division shall also send a copy of
the written report to the executive or the county council if requested to do so
by the complainant, if the director of the applicable division has not already
done so;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                c.  The department with
responsibility for the retaliatory action shall report back to the director of
the applicable division and complainant with an action plan for addressing the
retaliatory action and provide reasonable timelines for when the corrective
actions will occur.  The department's response should be made within fourteen
days of receipt of the director of the applicable division report;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                d.  If the director of the
applicable division deems that the responsible department has not taken
satisfactory action within a reasonable timeframe, the director of the
applicable division shall report the director of the applicable division's
determination to the executive and the county council; and</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>                e.  The director of the
applicable division may impose a fine on the department within which the
retaliatory action occurred; the director of the applicable division shall not
impose a fine greater than ten thousand dollars.  A fine should be imposed for
retaliatory actions where the department's response to the retaliatory actions
was grossly inadequate.  The department shall be given a reasonable opportunity
to be heard before imposition of any fine.  Proceeds collected from any fine
shall be deposited into an account to be used for the purpose of educating
employees about this chapter or applied by the department toward administrative
leave paid to the complainant where the reason for the administrative leave is
related to the retaliation claim.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            G.1.  At any stage in the
investigation, the director of the applicable division within the office of
public complaints or prosecutor may, with the agreement of the parties,
recommend, arrange for, convene or conduct voluntary mediation between the
employee and the subject of the investigation and/or agency head.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  If the employer and employee
reach agreement as a result of a mediation, the investigation shall be closed
and the employee shall not be entitled to seek a hearing under subsection I. of
this section.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  If the employer and employee
fail to reach agreement, the response times from subsection C. of this section
shall be tolled for the duration of the mediation process.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              4.  Mediation and other informal
resolution processes are voluntary.  No employer or employee shall be pressured
into participating in such processes, and no negative inferences shall be drawn
if any party declines to participate in such processes.  If a party agrees to
participate in voluntary mediation or other informal resolution process, that
party is under no obligation to accept the resolution recommended by the
mediator, the director of the applicable division, or any other person
participating in this process, and no negative inferences shall be drawn as a
result of a refusal to accept such recommendations.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            H.  The director of the applicable
division within the office of public complaints or prosecutor may close an
investigation at any time the director of the applicable division or prosecutor
determines that no further action is warranted and shall so notify the
complainant, the subject or subjects of the investigation and the agency head.  The
director of the applicable division or prosecutor shall also issue any reports
as required by this section.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            I.  Decisions of the director of
the applicable division within the office of public complaints under this
section may not be appealed to the board of ethics.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            J.  If an employee who has filed a
complaint of retaliation under this section is dissatisfied with the progress
of the investigation or the response and desires a hearing , the employee shall
deliver a request for hearing to the head of the branch within which
retaliation is alleged to have occurred within the later of:  one year of when
the alleged retaliation occurred or the employee reasonably should have known
of the occurrence; or ninety days from receipt of the department's response
under K.C.C. 3.42.060E.  The employee shall notify the office of public
complaints of the request.  Within five working days of receipt of the request
for hearing, the county shall apply to the state office of administrative
hearings for a hearing to be conducted as provided in RCW 42.41.040(4) through
(9).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            K.  An employee shall not have the
right to seek a hearing under this section if the complaint of retaliation is
pursued under and falls within the subject matter jurisdiction of a collective
bargaining agreement grievance procedure ending in binding arbitration or the
career service grievance procedure ending in a hearing before the personnel
board.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            L.  To the extent allowed by law,
investigating officials are encouraged to enter into cooperative agreements or
arrangements for receiving and processing complaints with other agencies or
entities that are investigating related complaints, so that duplication of
functions shall be minimized and multiple redundant investigations avoided.  (Ord.
20101 § 38, 2026:  <span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 18618 </span></span><span
lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 101, 2017:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 16580 § 8, 2009:  Ord. 11687 § 7, 1995).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<h1><a name="_Toc94867929"></a><a name="_Toc151386338"><span style='font-size:
12.0pt'>3.46    DRUG/ALCOHOL EDUCATION AND TESTING</span></a></h1>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Sections</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.46.010         Drug/alcohol
education and testing program - established.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.46.020         Definitions.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.46.030         Policy - limited
applicability.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.46.040         Education.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.46.050         Testing.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.46.060         Types of testing.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.46.070         Discipline.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.46.080         Executive
authorized to sign agreements.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.46.010  Drug/alcohol education
and testing program - established.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>  There is established a program for prohibited
drug use/alcohol misuse education and testing program policy limited to persons
employed by King County or employed by a transit contractor performing certain
safety sensitive functions as defined in applicable federal law.  (Ord. 12413 §
2, 1996).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.46.020  Definitions.</span></b></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            A.  &quot;Safety sensitive&quot;
relates to the following functions:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  Operating revenue service
vehicles, including operation when the vehicle is not in revenue service;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Operating nonrevenue service
vehicles when operation of such vehicles requires the driver to hold a
commercial driver's license (CDL);</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  Controlling the dispatch or
movement of a revenue service vehicle;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              4.  Maintaining a revenue service
vehicle or equipment used in revenue service;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              5.  Carrying a firearm for
transit security purposes; or</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              6.  All employees of independent
contractors who perform services for King County in positions which are listed
previously.  (Ord. 12413 § 1, 1996).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.46.030  Policy - limited
applicability.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'> 
King County as a federal grantee and contractor responsible for certain
transportation or related operations and programs is committed to maintaining a
drug/alcohol-free workplace to promote both the safety of its employees,
customers and the public and the quality of its services.  Every King County
employee or employee of a transit contractor who holds a position which is
defined as safety sensitive is subject to regulations issued pursuant to the
Omnibus Transportation Act of 1991, as subsequently amended, and each employee,
in accordance with this act and under King County authority shall follow
applicable rules and regulations promulgated under K.C.C. 2.98 and/or bargained
in good faith under Ordinance 11480, provided that such rules and regulations
shall not limit collectively bargained agreements already in effect upon August
19, 1996, codified in this chapter.  It is the policy of King County that the
provisions of this chapter shall be strictly related to the intent of the
Omnibus Transportation Employee Testing Act of 1991, as subsequently amended;
49 CFR Parts 40, 382, 653 and 654 and the U.S. Drug Free Workplace Act of 1988,
as subsequently amended and applicable regulations issued pursuant to such
acts.  The passage of Ordinance 12413 notwithstanding, nothing in this chapter
shall preclude a future legal challenge enjoining the federal government from
abrogating the civil rights protections contained in the State of Washington
Constitution.  (Ord. 12413 § 3, 1996).</span></p>

<p class=tx><span style='font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.46.040  Education.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>  All county employees
and employees of independent contractors who perform safety sensitive functions
covered by this chapter shall receive a copy of the county's prohibited drug
use and alcohol misuse education and testing program policy and appropriate
information concerning the prohibited drug and misuse education and testing
program.  The executive shall be authorized to establish an education program
appropriate to the functions of employees covered by this chapter.  (Ord. 12413
§ 4, 1996).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:-.1pt'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.46.050  Testing.  </span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Employees subject to
alcohol testing under this chapter will have a sample of their breath tested
for the presence of the intoxicating agent in beverage alcohol, ethyl alcohol
or other low molecular weight alcohols and including methyl or isopropyl alcohol. 
Any refusal to submit to an alcohol test and all positive alcohol tests will be
reported to the executive or designee.  Employees subject to drug testing will
have a<b> </b>sample of their urine tested for the presence of five drugs as
follows:</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              1.  Marijuana;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              2.  Cocaine;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              3.  Opiates;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              4.  Amphetamines;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>              5.  Phencyclidine.</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            All drug tests will be reported by
the testing laboratory to a medical review officer designated by the county who
will evaluate the results.  After evaluation and interpretation, all verified
positive test results will be reported by the medical review officer to the
executive or designee.  Any refusal to submit to a drug test will be
immediately reported by the collection site to the executive or designee.  If
employees test positive as previously explained, said employees will be
notified by the medical review officer that they have seventy-two hours
following this notification in which to request, at their own expense, that a
split urine specimen be tested by another laboratory certified by the state
Department of Health and Human Services.  In the event that the split sample
test is negative, the employee will be reimbursed for the test.  Failure to
request testing of the split specimen within seventy-two hours of being
notified of a positive test by the medical review officer will result in the
test results from the original specimen being accepted as the final test
results.  Provided, that there will be only one random testing pool for all
King County employees covered by the provisions of this chapter.  Independent
contractors will have the option of participating in one random testing pool
for all their employees who perform safety sensitive functions covered by this
chapter.  (<span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 18618 </span></span><span
lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 103, 2017:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 12413 § 5, 1996).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.46.060  Types of testing.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>  All county employees
subject to the provisions of this chapter shall be required to submit to the
following tests as applicable:  pre-employment tests; post accident tests;
random tests; reasonable suspicion tests; and return to duty/follow-up tests.  The
executive shall promulgate rules and regulations necessary and proper to
implement this chapter insuring employee confidentiality, the integrity of the
testing process, safeguarding the validity of the test results, and ensuring
that the test results are attributed to the correct employee.  Provided, that
all county employees and their union representatives, if applicable, subject to
the provisions of this chapter shall be provided with a copy of the appropriate
forms prepared indicating the grounds for requiring an employee to submit to a
reasonable suspicion test within twenty-four hours of testing or as soon  as 
possible  thereafter.  Provided  further, that when available, a second
supervisor will also observe an employee to determine if a reasonable suspicion
test is required and complete the related forms in accordance with this chapter
and rules and regulations promulgated pursuant thereof.  (Ord. 12413 § 6,
1996).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.46.070  Discipline.</span></b><span
style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>  It is the policy of
King County that employees performing safety sensitive<b> </b>functions as
defined in this chapter, as subsequently amended, will be subject to discipline
as appropriate and specified in procedures and rules promulgated under K.C.C.
2.98 and collective bargaining agreements adopted under Ordinance 11480. 
(Ord.  12413 § 7, 1996).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>            3.46.080  Executive authorized to
sign agreements.</span></b><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'> 
The executive or designee is authorized to enter into agreements with alcohol
and drug testing services providers as required for the implementation of this
chapter.  (<span style='letter-spacing:-.1pt'>Ord. 18618 </span></span><span
lang=DE style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;letter-spacing:
-.1pt'>§</span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;font-family:"Arial",sans-serif;
letter-spacing:-.1pt'> 104, 2017:  </span><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>Ord. 12413 § 8, 1996).</span></p>

<p class=MsoNormal style='text-align:justify'><span style='font-size:12.0pt;
font-family:"Arial",sans-serif'>&nbsp;</span></p>

</div>

</body>

</html>
